<!-- cell: character id="e19c3118-15cd-4a2a-b0be-62fac7918164" title="Markus Vader" concepts="61d2ce61-6bb0-42d0-935a-855dcfb2b93c, 4a6c9794-79b8-471c-9251-494040817e01" -->

A miner on the scavenger spaceship Victoria.

He comes from UK on Earth, and after a difficult adolescence he signed up with the Bolo Extraction corporation and started flying to the asteroid belt.

He doesn't have a family, puts up with danger, and wants to be left alone. He's like a rock other build on.

Motivation: freedom

Traits: eager to please, swears, blackmailer

Looks: stout, glowing tattoos, long coat

<!-- cell: character id="4f2073f2-a7bf-4301-b942-345126d40ae3" title="Dr Elen Markovsky" concepts="4a6c9794-79b8-471c-9251-494040817e01, abf702ed-3912-4556-846b-2e498c05652c" -->

An exobiologist working in University of the City of London, UK. She studies microbacterial organisms retrieved during space excavation.

The human knowledge and discovery on the matter hasn't really progressed much since the start of the space exploration age, and the most complex samples retrieved ad studies were mineral deposits indicative of excrements of single-cell organisms - but those single-cell organisms were never found.

She's a mother of two girls, 6 and 8 years old, and married to a news anchor in the national TV.

<!-- cell: character id="36bdaad6-61da-42d5-84cf-aaecb586068e" title="Bagor Vavran" concepts="abf702ed-3912-4556-846b-2e498c05652c, 61d2ce61-6bb0-42d0-935a-855dcfb2b93c, 4a6c9794-79b8-471c-9251-494040817e01" -->

Owner of the Bolo Extraction corporation. A vicious Bulgarian with ties to russian mafia who got into power withg ruthless murders and extortion.

Bolo Extraction is a legitimate business and a large corporation, but the employees are exploited to the maximum, work in very unsafe conditions - all orchestrated and arranged by Bagor, who wants to get richer and richer.

Bagor has a wife, Jamelia, of Jamaican descent - very beautiful trophy wife, and no children. He parades Jamelia and treats her like a trophy.

He is a brutal narcissist and a sociopath.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="61d2ce61-6bb0-42d0-935a-855dcfb2b93c" title="mining resources in outer space" -->

The extraction of minerals, water ice and rare elements from asteroids, moons and planets to sustain and enrich human civilization beyond Earth. The story shows it as both a lifeline and a lever of power: whoever controls the drills and claims on Mars controls the colony's future. This makes the digging that uncovers something no one expected all the more consequential.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="4a6c9794-79b8-471c-9251-494040817e01" title="finding an alien artifact on mars" -->

A buried object of clearly non-human origin, unearthed during routine excavation, that overturns the assumption that humanity is alone and that Mars is just a rock to be mined. Its unknown purpose, age and makers drive the mystery. The reader wants to learn what it is, what it does, and whether the rivals scrambling to claim it understand what they are fighting over.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="abf702ed-3912-4556-846b-2e498c05652c" title="political squabbles between human factions" -->

The rivalries, alliances and power struggles between competing groups, such as corporations, governments and colonists, each pursuing its own interests at the expense of shared goals. The story shows these divisions as petty and dangerous in the face of something truly alien. Mining rights and the race to control the artifact expose how readily humans turn on one another even at the edge of the unknown.

<!-- cell: plot id="970ef795-7d78-4910-a805-b31ca9aeee94" title="mining resources in outer space" edges="623b6254-73c4-49a4-a8a8-ea454907de2c:d56007c1-3b7a-4c61-940d-abd50c7f8ab3>a5c99e77-5104-488b-a34b-f82a7a7a852e, ad1b099b-2838-4c65-9f79-1ba4145782c5:a5c99e77-5104-488b-a34b-f82a7a7a852e>e7f74db3-9a21-4321-b905-9ca8dfdf678d, 2219a989-ceb8-4b0c-b363-d9721225724f:e7f74db3-9a21-4321-b905-9ca8dfdf678d>b1c21e22-4a2f-4185-b24f-0a7c8912021e, 8fb0e205-5f95-4930-a7e7-3ef0a3bf3483:b1c21e22-4a2f-4185-b24f-0a7c8912021e>be8861eb-84e9-40b1-abca-9052572a8993, 4f9c64a4-f6c1-422c-9c59-e86adbc09f90:be8861eb-84e9-40b1-abca-9052572a8993>d807c3be-c14e-4f79-8514-0b96ee919915, 1d973490-288b-4084-bde4-2d65c4d665aa:d807c3be-c14e-4f79-8514-0b96ee919915>56ba5971-b6a1-4b83-80c0-4427d59c2ba1, 91db1fe3-d05f-465c-84b7-b810aa6f59b7:56ba5971-b6a1-4b83-80c0-4427d59c2ba1>1ceec3b9-ad33-4018-83de-7c5895b3df59, 12a76dbf-311b-4773-9583-1dd856a28a07:1ceec3b9-ad33-4018-83de-7c5895b3df59>e98ce588-529a-4a40-9ec3-8375d9f4442d, e0d33cd8-a7f0-4988-9abc-f8d8c0fb50c0:e98ce588-529a-4a40-9ec3-8375d9f4442d>75ecb875-cdeb-4beb-ae59-14ffd4d7d5a5, 7e280215-0218-4dab-927c-a550ec13ebef:75ecb875-cdeb-4beb-ae59-14ffd4d7d5a5>ebc0d7bc-2c2d-4559-944e-471c0bbc0417, d08f377d-6f88-4b22-a778-fd10ad65a35e:ebc0d7bc-2c2d-4559-944e-471c0bbc0417>93e4ac09-6be8-4109-b3bf-2a9a7c493b48" concepts="61d2ce61-6bb0-42d0-935a-855dcfb2b93c" characters="e19c3118-15cd-4a2a-b0be-62fac7918164, 36bdaad6-61da-42d5-84cf-aaecb586068e, 48531820-f25e-452e-8b5a-c0e785daf28f, f3f32f7b-5874-4559-8192-497fcf8af228, 3a8f2b8f-437f-4657-9d6c-8f10970c68fe" -->

<ol>
<li id="d56007c1-3b7a-4c61-940d-abd50c7f8ab3" timestamp="1.1" place="7da064ef-7bd8-4a5d-bcab-7c332bb1e40e" characters="e19c3118-15cd-4a2a-b0be-62fac7918164, 36bdaad6-61da-42d5-84cf-aaecb586068e" words="0-381">Bagor appears on a holo-call and orders the Victoria off its asteroid run to Mars, where Bolo Extraction has seized the drilling claims under the colony's only ice field, because whoever controls that water controls the colony; Markus still owes years on his contract, so he has no choice but to go.</li>
<li id="a5c99e77-5104-488b-a34b-f82a7a7a852e" timestamp="2" place="5a324932-47f9-4970-9866-a8240b54efaa" characters="e19c3118-15cd-4a2a-b0be-62fac7918164, 36bdaad6-61da-42d5-84cf-aaecb586068e, 48531820-f25e-452e-8b5a-c0e785daf28f, 315e8368-00af-469e-95cd-09210ff59434, e0e45800-cf0a-4c4a-94bf-9d50306ffe24, 2a67ead4-91ec-41a1-bb8e-a47608c52152, 43366964-6e56-470b-bed5-3ebec4d93645" words="407-976">The Victoria lands to find Bolo guards fencing off the ice field while settlers queue at dry water pumps. Tomas Reyes, Markus Vader's new shift lead, walks him past rigs already running hot and warns that Bagor Vavran has brought in the Victoria's crew to squeeze the colony even harder. In the council hall Bagor waves the seized claim deeds as proof that the colony drinks only what he lets them dig. Then rumours leak from Dmitri Kolev's crew that something was found, Tomas files a claim that shaft 7 lies on settler land, and Colonel Ines Haldane announces a government decree to take it for the state. Bagor makes Kolev swear before the council that nothing was found, then vows to prove the structure's value in secret and outmanoeuvre both rivals.</li>
<li id="e7f74db3-9a21-4321-b905-9ca8dfdf678d" timestamp="3" place="c4beee34-73ea-46be-939a-5e54a5c8f4c4" characters="e19c3118-15cd-4a2a-b0be-62fac7918164, 36bdaad6-61da-42d5-84cf-aaecb586068e, 48531820-f25e-452e-8b5a-c0e785daf28f, 0a75f0e7-bfb8-4b81-9230-3d058f6d9206" words="1010-1508">When the Victoria's crew joins the drill site, Bagor Vavran triples the extraction quota and cuts the colony's water ration so the settlers must buy back their own ice at Bolo prices, and Tomas Reyes, the site's medic, tells Markus Vader he cannot walk off without forfeiting the credits for his daughter's passage, so Markus overdrives the rigs and sinks the shaft deeper than any safe limit. From that depth the drill brings up a shard that Markus hides in his bunk, and by morning it has slowly regrown a tiny lattice branch. When Tomas spots it and calls it "not rock, not ours," Markus pays him to keep quiet, smuggles it past Bolo's cargo scan onto the Victoria for the flight home, and resolves to seek out the one scientist who studies traces of life from space, Dr Elen Markovsky in London.</li>
<li id="b1c21e22-4a2f-4185-b24f-0a7c8912021e" timestamp="4.3" place="e3c9b30c-e2ff-464e-bf3d-8069326ab207" characters="e19c3118-15cd-4a2a-b0be-62fac7918164, 36bdaad6-61da-42d5-84cf-aaecb586068e, 48531820-f25e-452e-8b5a-c0e785daf28f" words="1553-2079">Deep below the safe limit, the overdriven bit grinds against a layer of smooth, impossibly hard rock beneath the ice, and when Tomas asks to halt, Bagor on the holo-link threatens to void his contract and the colony's water deliveries unless they cut through, so Markus wires a hidden recorder into his coat before ordering the crew to bore on.</li>
<li id="be8861eb-84e9-40b1-abca-9052572a8993" timestamp="5" place="08db182a-6d9a-4291-9624-9a9ec46c6a9c" characters="e19c3118-15cd-4a2a-b0be-62fac7918164, f3f32f7b-5874-4559-8192-497fcf8af228, 3a8f2b8f-437f-4657-9d6c-8f10970c68fe, 4f2073f2-a7bf-4301-b942-345126d40ae3, 315e8368-00af-469e-95cd-09210ff59434, 36bdaad6-61da-42d5-84cf-aaecb586068e, 2a67ead4-91ec-41a1-bb8e-a47608c52152, e0e45800-cf0a-4c4a-94bf-9d50306ffe24, 0a75f0e7-bfb8-4b81-9230-3d058f6d9206, 43366964-6e56-470b-bed5-3ebec4d93645" words="2106-2772">When the overdriven bit shatters on the hard layer, Bagor Vavran's enforcer Dragan Petrov arrives to restart the drills before the colony's ice quota slips, so Markus Vader confronts Ines Okafor with recordings of her ice skimming until she hands over an off-books hand rig, and he carries it down the shaft and cuts through. Kolev secretly slips a core from the now-sealed shaft to Dr Elen Markovsky, newly arrived at the depot's makeshift lab to study the broadcast find, and she discovers living microbes in it, the first ever recovered. Bagor, Colonel Haldane and Tomas Reyk each corner her among the supply crates, demanding she certify the find as their own property and offering money, state authority and colonial loyalty. She resolves instead to plead for shared access at the emergency council hearing.</li>
<li id="d807c3be-c14e-4f79-8514-0b96ee919915" timestamp="6" place="e5cd8c57-0cc1-4f89-9f41-cb34fa1ac290" characters="e19c3118-15cd-4a2a-b0be-62fac7918164, 36bdaad6-61da-42d5-84cf-aaecb586068e, 48531820-f25e-452e-8b5a-c0e785daf28f, 4f2073f2-a7bf-4301-b942-345126d40ae3, 0a75f0e7-bfb8-4b81-9230-3d058f6d9206" words="2820-3491">Pushing the drills past safe limits to strip the colony's ice field, Markus's crew breaks into a sealed chamber that is not natural. Bagor, on the holo-link, orders the shaft collapsed with Tomas and the other witnesses still inside so nothing can threaten his claims. Markus quietly records both the chamber and the order, pockets a shard, and scrambles out to Dr Elen Markovsky's site lab. Elen finds the lattice is sealed and layered with the same mineral traces left by the single-celled organisms no one has ever found. When Markus uses her findings and his recording to blackmail Bagor for his freedom, Bagor decides the warm lattice must be an alien power source and forces them both back down to dig out the rest.</li>
<li id="56ba5971-b6a1-4b83-80c0-4427d59c2ba1" timestamp="6.5" place="00d0c245-3b2f-4e78-b1a8-859113c76481" characters="e19c3118-15cd-4a2a-b0be-62fac7918164, 36bdaad6-61da-42d5-84cf-aaecb586068e, 48531820-f25e-452e-8b5a-c0e785daf28f, f3f32f7b-5874-4559-8192-497fcf8af228, 19d4d1f6-b096-4ed8-a6b3-7d3ca629ea7c" words="3542-4028">Markus picks up a faint suit beacon from Tomas under the rubble, but Bagor has locked every drill on the claim to his personal codes so no one can dig, so Markus blackmails depot quartermaster Ines Okafor over her skimmed ice shipments into handing him an unregistered hand rig, then hides copies of his recordings on a dead-man's switch.</li>
<li id="1ceec3b9-ad33-4018-83de-7c5895b3df59" timestamp="8" place="7b19cf13-902a-49bc-af7d-b4a33f4e8693" characters="e19c3118-15cd-4a2a-b0be-62fac7918164, 3a8f2b8f-437f-4657-9d6c-8f10970c68fe, 48531820-f25e-452e-8b5a-c0e785daf28f, 4f2073f2-a7bf-4301-b942-345126d40ae3, 315e8368-00af-469e-95cd-09210ff59434, 36bdaad6-61da-42d5-84cf-aaecb586068e, 2a67ead4-91ec-41a1-bb8e-a47608c52152, e0e45800-cf0a-4c4a-94bf-9d50306ffe24, 43366964-6e56-470b-bed5-3ebec4d93645" words="4060-4678">Markus Vader slips past Bagor Vavran's enforcer Dragan Petrov at shift change and bores down the collapsed shaft toward trapped Tomas Reyes's beacon with the hand rig. Its core readings hit a smooth, unnatural wall buried in the ice, revealing a sealed chamber beside Tomas that makes the claim worth far more than its ice and gives Markus his leverage over Bagor. On a company radio he hears the factions bargain. Bagor offers Tomas's settler militia the colony's water back if they turn on Colonel Ines Haldane's troops. Haldane declares Bolo's charter void and promises the settlers land rights if they help seize the mine. Tomas pretends to bargain with both over his beacon link while sending his men forward. The pacts collapse within minutes as all three sides race for the chamber at once. Dmitri Kolev, hauling the drill rig behind Bolo security, warns Markus to hurry, so Markus kicks out the rusted grate of an old ventilation shaft and drops toward the artifact.</li>
<li id="e98ce588-529a-4a40-9ec3-8375d9f4442d" timestamp="9" place="77899cf1-e11a-49e2-9149-5b3da6479ead" characters="e19c3118-15cd-4a2a-b0be-62fac7918164, 48531820-f25e-452e-8b5a-c0e785daf28f, 36bdaad6-61da-42d5-84cf-aaecb586068e, 4f2073f2-a7bf-4301-b942-345126d40ae3, 0a75f0e7-bfb8-4b81-9230-3d058f6d9206" words="4728-5218">Markus and Dr Elen Markovsky take a stolen hand rig back into the collapsed shaft, cut through the rubble and find Tomas Reyes alive in an air pocket beside a sealed chamber housing the full artifact, a huge warm lattice far older than any human record, and Elen warns that its slow pulsing heat looks like something kept alive, not a battery; Markus sends Bagor Vavran a clip of the recordings and a message that copies sit on a dead-man's switch to the colony council, summoning him to the chamber, where Bagor dismisses Elen's warning and orders the chamber breached at once so he can claim his power source, while Markus quietly bargains with him for the claims and a contract release in exchange for leading the breach.</li>
<li id="75ecb875-cdeb-4beb-ae59-14ffd4d7d5a5" timestamp="8.4" place="8e2ea47b-7b61-4323-97c9-1cc99435e238" characters="e19c3118-15cd-4a2a-b0be-62fac7918164, 48531820-f25e-452e-8b5a-c0e785daf28f, 36bdaad6-61da-42d5-84cf-aaecb586068e, 3a8f2b8f-437f-4657-9d6c-8f10970c68fe" words="5254-5791">Bagor arrives with his enforcer Dragan and orders the overdriven rigs restarted to bury the shaft for good, but Markus hauls Tomas out alive before the crew and shows that the dead-man's switch is live, so Bagor calls off the drills and follows Markus down to the sealed chamber to bargain.</li>
<li id="ebc0d7bc-2c2d-4559-944e-471c0bbc0417" timestamp="11" place="610aeeb9-367b-42a0-bb54-c1cc6d40d65f" characters="e19c3118-15cd-4a2a-b0be-62fac7918164, 36bdaad6-61da-42d5-84cf-aaecb586068e, 3a8f2b8f-437f-4657-9d6c-8f10970c68fe, 4f2073f2-a7bf-4301-b942-345126d40ae3, 2a67ead4-91ec-41a1-bb8e-a47608c52152, e0e45800-cf0a-4c4a-94bf-9d50306ffe24, 315e8368-00af-469e-95cd-09210ff59434, 43366964-6e56-470b-bed5-3ebec4d93645, 0a75f0e7-bfb8-4b81-9230-3d058f6d9206" words="5817-6452">Before the alien shell the drill broke into, Bagor sends Dragan back up the shaft and offers Markus a cut of the Mars claims to keep quiet, boasting that whoever owns the drills owns the colony and that he ordered the shaft buried with the witnesses inside, unaware that the recorder under Markus's coat is capturing every word. Then gunfire erupts between Colonel Haldane's troops and Reyk's colonist militia. Dr Elen Markovsky drags the wounded Kolev behind the drill rig and sees the artifact's shell begin closing around the drill wound as its microbial signal fades, so she begs everyone to cease fire. But Haldane will not stand down unless the militia disarms first, Reyk will not unless Bolo and the troops withdraw, and Bagor orders his security to retake the drill, so the shooting spreads across the chamber.</li>
<li id="93e4ac09-6be8-4109-b3bf-2a9a7c493b48" timestamp="12" place="93413ad3-a2a5-4342-a102-afac171e1dd3" characters="e19c3118-15cd-4a2a-b0be-62fac7918164, 36bdaad6-61da-42d5-84cf-aaecb586068e, 4f2073f2-a7bf-4301-b942-345126d40ae3, 0a75f0e7-bfb8-4b81-9230-3d058f6d9206, 86dc897a-e3c2-4b0b-8730-00395393827d" words="6496-14005">Markus Vader confronts Bagor Vavran in the strange sealed chamber the drill broke into. Markus carries recordings of the chamber and of Bagor's orders to bury it along with the miners who saw it. Ignoring him, Bagor forces the artifact open to sell it as an alien power source. It proves instead to be a dormant seed vault, and it releases its ancient single-celled makers into the Martian soil. Dr Elen Markovsky recognizes them as the organisms behind the fossil traces no one could ever find. Left with nothing he can own, Bagor is forced by Markus's recordings to hand the Mars claims to the colony council and cancel Markus's contract, and Markus walks away from the drills a free man.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: plot id="78bac125-33e1-4c7d-a7a5-7aadb37340d7" title="finding an alien artifact on mars" edges="6dc98acd-985a-472e-8dfa-faa8fdae69f3:7f1643b6-3965-4699-af8f-4e60165369a5>0c9a6f18-11b0-4aa2-8461-b12f8e3f31c9, f15ce76b-0bbb-401e-9328-0c1968ea415a:0c9a6f18-11b0-4aa2-8461-b12f8e3f31c9>d09a4c8d-31f1-4fc4-9c46-813ee7f5d7e8, ff15a2ed-fe2c-4676-ad3a-9ad54840b9bc:d09a4c8d-31f1-4fc4-9c46-813ee7f5d7e8>d226e2fe-9aa6-4c3a-b56e-0458b54a1e61, 2b66a696-2346-456b-beec-f136b2ecfa79:d226e2fe-9aa6-4c3a-b56e-0458b54a1e61>98eb94cd-f886-4efc-83e7-cd251842adf6, 6621129a-5c86-459d-a20a-370a6e4ba6de:98eb94cd-f886-4efc-83e7-cd251842adf6>646a9715-6be6-47d7-bdbf-ecc45dbc8098, 59874025-9f0b-47db-8503-1517658a50a5:646a9715-6be6-47d7-bdbf-ecc45dbc8098>f248ba4e-c554-44cf-b024-2ffb5edebf34, 8d3651a0-caa5-43d7-a1ca-a0fcf4eae1db:f248ba4e-c554-44cf-b024-2ffb5edebf34>2b6b528a-123c-4358-a03e-77daaa604655, c8eb8fc6-14c5-4be4-82ef-9aee12fa4cae:2b6b528a-123c-4358-a03e-77daaa604655>9f745350-64d9-4150-a3a0-d51568ec6cb5, d99a770c-4b3e-4830-aa2d-31dbe6a038ba:9f745350-64d9-4150-a3a0-d51568ec6cb5>4b7c65b8-6ac3-4fab-88cd-6ad0cc1a2533, a6394a1d-3757-4fd4-8853-de311b8adcf8:4b7c65b8-6ac3-4fab-88cd-6ad0cc1a2533>fe245551-a7ed-4c56-b842-612fc5c8d021, a7d61125-bc77-4c7d-bf02-70a8b6a1443a:fe245551-a7ed-4c56-b842-612fc5c8d021>66ebc65a-47db-4f7e-986a-20f44680e1ee" concepts="4a6c9794-79b8-471c-9251-494040817e01" characters="e19c3118-15cd-4a2a-b0be-62fac7918164, 4f2073f2-a7bf-4301-b942-345126d40ae3, 36bdaad6-61da-42d5-84cf-aaecb586068e" -->

<ol>
<li id="7f1643b6-3965-4699-af8f-4e60165369a5" timestamp="1" place="4094d7e6-25b3-4cf1-8f95-cb20161a9756" characters="e19c3118-15cd-4a2a-b0be-62fac7918164, 36bdaad6-61da-42d5-84cf-aaecb586068e, 315e8368-00af-469e-95cd-09210ff59434" words="7040-7598">During a routine core drill by Foreman Dmitri Kolev's crew, Markus Vader's bit strikes a smooth, faintly warm black lattice that is not rock, buried far below layers that should hold nothing but stone. Bagor Vavran orders the find hidden from the Mars colonial council and the UN Space Authority so Bolo Extraction can claim sole mining rights over it, while Markus quietly pockets a sample instead of logging it, sensing it could buy his way out of Bolo for good.</li>
<li id="0c9a6f18-11b0-4aa2-8461-b12f8e3f31c9" timestamp="2.1" place="4094d7e6-25b3-4cf1-8f95-cb20161a9756" characters="e19c3118-15cd-4a2a-b0be-62fac7918164, 36bdaad6-61da-42d5-84cf-aaecb586068e" words="7649-8063">Bagor sees the drill's heat spike in the telemetry, calls the site, and orders the borehole sealed and the whole crew searched, calling the find "a new ore worth billions," so Markus tapes the warm shard inside his coat lining, passes the search, and boards the Victoria for home sure that Bagor has no idea what he is trying to own.</li>
<li id="d09a4c8d-31f1-4fc4-9c46-813ee7f5d7e8" timestamp="3" place="c4beee34-73ea-46be-939a-5e54a5c8f4c4" characters="e19c3118-15cd-4a2a-b0be-62fac7918164, 36bdaad6-61da-42d5-84cf-aaecb586068e, 48531820-f25e-452e-8b5a-c0e785daf28f, 0a75f0e7-bfb8-4b81-9230-3d058f6d9206" words="1010-1508">When the Victoria's crew joins the drill site, Bagor Vavran triples the extraction quota and cuts the colony's water ration so the settlers must buy back their own ice at Bolo prices, and Tomas Reyes, the site's medic, tells Markus Vader he cannot walk off without forfeiting the credits for his daughter's passage, so Markus overdrives the rigs and sinks the shaft deeper than any safe limit. From that depth the drill brings up a shard that Markus hides in his bunk, and by morning it has slowly regrown a tiny lattice branch. When Tomas spots it and calls it "not rock, not ours," Markus pays him to keep quiet, smuggles it past Bolo's cargo scan onto the Victoria for the flight home, and resolves to seek out the one scientist who studies traces of life from space, Dr Elen Markovsky in London.</li>
<li id="d226e2fe-9aa6-4c3a-b56e-0458b54a1e61" timestamp="4" place="62a5909c-d4c8-4cc8-b528-547490c5044c" characters="e19c3118-15cd-4a2a-b0be-62fac7918164, 4f2073f2-a7bf-4301-b942-345126d40ae3, 315e8368-00af-469e-95cd-09210ff59434, e0e45800-cf0a-4c4a-94bf-9d50306ffe24, 2a67ead4-91ec-41a1-bb8e-a47608c52152, 36bdaad6-61da-42d5-84cf-aaecb586068e, 0a75f0e7-bfb8-4b81-9230-3d058f6d9206, 43366964-6e56-470b-bed5-3ebec4d93645" words="8089-8701">As Kolev's sealed crew cycle out of the airlock, they smuggle drill footage of the buried structure to Reyk, who broadcasts it across the colony as proof Bolo is stealing settlers' land. Meanwhile Markus Vader corners Dr Elen Markovsky, fresh from her lecture at the colony's university annex, and shows her a shard the crew pried from the structure. She dismisses it as a crystal hoax until it grows warm in her hand and pushes out a new lattice branch before her eyes, so she agrees to examine it secretly in her lab after hours, telling no one, not even her news-anchor husband. Haldane seizes on the broadcast to demand an emergency council hearing to assert the state's claim, while Bagor threatens Kolev's job and his daughter's medical payments unless he swears the footage is fake, and Kolev refuses to say anything at all.</li>
<li id="98eb94cd-f886-4efc-83e7-cd251842adf6" timestamp="4.8" place="0a4676cb-8e60-4348-b67b-f3e2c66494d6" characters="e19c3118-15cd-4a2a-b0be-62fac7918164, 36bdaad6-61da-42d5-84cf-aaecb586068e, 883db39f-6217-47c1-820a-b3771df93ce9, 290d8b0f-af51-40f7-ba1b-77b1d0e0dc3a" words="8737-9322">Bagor calls Markus on video, furious that a core sample logged at the Mars dig has gone missing, and threatens him; Markus swears he knows nothing, then sees the shard pulsing faintly in time with his own heartbeat, so he wraps it in lead foil, sews it into his coat lining and slips past Bolo's watchers to take it to Elen's lab, deciding it is the leverage that will buy his freedom.</li>
<li id="646a9715-6be6-47d7-bdbf-ecc45dbc8098" timestamp="6" place="e5cd8c57-0cc1-4f89-9f41-cb34fa1ac290" characters="e19c3118-15cd-4a2a-b0be-62fac7918164, 36bdaad6-61da-42d5-84cf-aaecb586068e, 48531820-f25e-452e-8b5a-c0e785daf28f, 4f2073f2-a7bf-4301-b942-345126d40ae3, 0a75f0e7-bfb8-4b81-9230-3d058f6d9206" words="2820-3491">Pushing the drills past safe limits to strip the colony's ice field, Markus's crew breaks into a sealed chamber that is not natural. Bagor, on the holo-link, orders the shaft collapsed with Tomas and the other witnesses still inside so nothing can threaten his claims. Markus quietly records both the chamber and the order, pockets a shard, and scrambles out to Dr Elen Markovsky's site lab. Elen finds the lattice is sealed and layered with the same mineral traces left by the single-celled organisms no one has ever found. When Markus uses her findings and his recording to blackmail Bagor for his freedom, Bagor decides the warm lattice must be an alien power source and forces them both back down to dig out the rest.</li>
<li id="f248ba4e-c554-44cf-b024-2ffb5edebf34" timestamp="7" place="aa15d329-fac5-44de-a271-d2f8e3444aaf" characters="e19c3118-15cd-4a2a-b0be-62fac7918164, 4f2073f2-a7bf-4301-b942-345126d40ae3, 36bdaad6-61da-42d5-84cf-aaecb586068e, 2a67ead4-91ec-41a1-bb8e-a47608c52152, e0e45800-cf0a-4c4a-94bf-9d50306ffe24, 315e8368-00af-469e-95cd-09210ff59434, 0a75f0e7-bfb8-4b81-9230-3d058f6d9206, 43366964-6e56-470b-bed5-3ebec4d93645" words="9344-9968">When Elen sets the smuggled shard beside the ground scanner, it warms and pulses in step with a faint heat signal deep under the trench, revealing a much larger structure still buried there, and the three factions race for it. Bagor shuts off the colony's water pumps and seals the main lift to stall the militia, then orders Markus's crew to dig through the night despite the unsafe walls. Markus swears at him but agrees to drive the crew because he still hopes to buy his freedom. Meanwhile Colonel Haldane's troops commandeer the ore rail line and Reyk's militia blasts open a side tunnel. Kolev, ordered by Bagor to haul the drill rig down, quietly shows Elen an old ventilation shaft so she can reach the buried structure before the guns do.</li>
<li id="2b6b528a-123c-4358-a03e-77daaa604655" timestamp="7.0" place="a682c9e4-a6f4-4e44-81e2-ad0dba19a8c0" characters="e19c3118-15cd-4a2a-b0be-62fac7918164, 4f2073f2-a7bf-4301-b942-345126d40ae3, 36bdaad6-61da-42d5-84cf-aaecb586068e, 48531820-f25e-452e-8b5a-c0e785daf28f, 0a75f0e7-bfb8-4b81-9230-3d058f6d9206" words="10008-10542">Partway through the night dig an unsafe wall collapses onto crewman Tomas Reyes, and as Markus hauls him free the slide uncovers a smooth, warm curve of the buried structure. Elen sees its pulse quicken whenever the crew comes near, as if it senses them. Bagor orders them to keep digging without shoring, and Markus secretly records the order to use against him later.</li>
<li id="9f745350-64d9-4150-a3a0-d51568ec6cb5" timestamp="9" place="77899cf1-e11a-49e2-9149-5b3da6479ead" characters="e19c3118-15cd-4a2a-b0be-62fac7918164, 48531820-f25e-452e-8b5a-c0e785daf28f, 36bdaad6-61da-42d5-84cf-aaecb586068e, 4f2073f2-a7bf-4301-b942-345126d40ae3, 0a75f0e7-bfb8-4b81-9230-3d058f6d9206" words="4728-5218">Markus and Dr Elen Markovsky take a stolen hand rig back into the collapsed shaft, cut through the rubble and find Tomas Reyes alive in an air pocket beside a sealed chamber housing the full artifact, a huge warm lattice far older than any human record, and Elen warns that its slow pulsing heat looks like something kept alive, not a battery; Markus sends Bagor Vavran a clip of the recordings and a message that copies sit on a dead-man's switch to the colony council, summoning him to the chamber, where Bagor dismisses Elen's warning and orders the chamber breached at once so he can claim his power source, while Markus quietly bargains with him for the claims and a contract release in exchange for leading the breach.</li>
<li id="4b7c65b8-6ac3-4fab-88cd-6ad0cc1a2533" timestamp="10" place="41a6f298-d99f-473e-84ff-0e5f3acfc86a" characters="e19c3118-15cd-4a2a-b0be-62fac7918164, 4f2073f2-a7bf-4301-b942-345126d40ae3, 36bdaad6-61da-42d5-84cf-aaecb586068e, 2a67ead4-91ec-41a1-bb8e-a47608c52152, e0e45800-cf0a-4c4a-94bf-9d50306ffe24, 315e8368-00af-469e-95cd-09210ff59434, 0a75f0e7-bfb8-4b81-9230-3d058f6d9206, 43366964-6e56-470b-bed5-3ebec4d93645" words="10571-11147">Markus leads the breach and finds a sealed inner core whose shell is crusted with the same mineral residue Elen has studied for years in fossil samples. She begs them to stop, warning that the lattice is guarding something living and that the microbes are withdrawing into the core. Markus has secretly recorded Bagor ordering the breach over her warning, so he blackmails Bagor into signing the contract release and steps aside, leaving Bagor to force the core open himself. Bagor reminds Kolev of his daughter's medical debt, and Kolev starts the drill. Meanwhile Haldane reads out a state seizure decree, Reyk shouts the colony's claim and Bagor waves Bolo's mining charter. As the bit bites into the shell, a militiaman fires at Kolev and wounds him, and Haldane's troops and Bolo security return fire.</li>
<li id="fe245551-a7ed-4c56-b842-612fc5c8d021" timestamp="9.1" place="41a6f298-d99f-473e-84ff-0e5f3acfc86a" characters="e19c3118-15cd-4a2a-b0be-62fac7918164, 4f2073f2-a7bf-4301-b942-345126d40ae3, 36bdaad6-61da-42d5-84cf-aaecb586068e, 0a75f0e7-bfb8-4b81-9230-3d058f6d9206" words="11187-11737">Bagor cuts the core's outer seal with a plasma torch and the artifact begins to hum and warm as the station's moist air leaks in, which he takes as proof of an alien power source worth billions. Elen scrapes the fresh residue and finds it is still forming, showing the lattice is an incubator waking up rather than a machine, but Markus, keeping his recording safe and his release signed, holds her back and lets Bagor pry the inner seam wider.</li>
<li id="66ebc65a-47db-4f7e-986a-20f44680e1ee" timestamp="12" place="93413ad3-a2a5-4342-a102-afac171e1dd3" characters="e19c3118-15cd-4a2a-b0be-62fac7918164, 36bdaad6-61da-42d5-84cf-aaecb586068e, 4f2073f2-a7bf-4301-b942-345126d40ae3, 0a75f0e7-bfb8-4b81-9230-3d058f6d9206, 86dc897a-e3c2-4b0b-8730-00395393827d" words="6496-14005">Markus Vader confronts Bagor Vavran in the strange sealed chamber the drill broke into. Markus carries recordings of the chamber and of Bagor's orders to bury it along with the miners who saw it. Ignoring him, Bagor forces the artifact open to sell it as an alien power source. It proves instead to be a dormant seed vault, and it releases its ancient single-celled makers into the Martian soil. Dr Elen Markovsky recognizes them as the organisms behind the fossil traces no one could ever find. Left with nothing he can own, Bagor is forced by Markus's recordings to hand the Mars claims to the colony council and cancel Markus's contract, and Markus walks away from the drills a free man.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: plot id="a7702b43-af53-46f2-8858-a95212ebba22" title="political squabbles between human factions" edges="d2c40953-630d-4bde-b602-03a049db30d1:973aeec3-cc9f-4597-a9ed-c5db52cf0fb3>589e8ae4-f0cf-4567-ab11-eea32e09271d, 6e1c7df8-3fe8-4b88-b415-44bf534ac348:589e8ae4-f0cf-4567-ab11-eea32e09271d>f3d306d5-2d16-43e6-8790-6ca5f6fc4586, e62f443e-540e-4ac3-a301-e619ddaa9e8b:f3d306d5-2d16-43e6-8790-6ca5f6fc4586>932762de-dfa2-40f4-9281-b762b2ad6490, e36d53bd-3143-496a-aa71-6d1691710597:932762de-dfa2-40f4-9281-b762b2ad6490>f70f492b-df47-4518-8bd0-3eef4d039803, b14e6d06-92e9-4a00-9af4-7da5db1933b0:f70f492b-df47-4518-8bd0-3eef4d039803>1e17dc5e-2f5f-43fa-a2bb-30add038aa84, ddb837e2-87db-4d67-b07a-7134ab1a72b9:1e17dc5e-2f5f-43fa-a2bb-30add038aa84>c21799d3-52cb-437f-b175-6fee0c01aeba, c02edcd0-408e-4dd4-8009-5b94bac624b4:c21799d3-52cb-437f-b175-6fee0c01aeba>b4350c14-dfb5-4835-8827-475cac688f62, 4b342c6a-9430-46ac-89fa-490c9ce2f45c:b4350c14-dfb5-4835-8827-475cac688f62>963a13ed-0250-4f5c-abad-2eaee63842e8, ba0a1d00-7d0b-44ec-8f5d-a145c13e8a88:963a13ed-0250-4f5c-abad-2eaee63842e8>381a00ef-2229-4af2-bafe-0e67de5dc654, 3cd412b8-1bfa-4ed0-a0fd-da6b1a0dcfb8:381a00ef-2229-4af2-bafe-0e67de5dc654>0cccde3c-eeaa-4fc4-a0e5-bd74ff1aadce, 015c05a3-e1b7-4027-b341-2350052c1b58:0cccde3c-eeaa-4fc4-a0e5-bd74ff1aadce>2cd21a9c-3994-47d0-85f0-658fb95e324c" concepts="abf702ed-3912-4556-846b-2e498c05652c" characters="4f2073f2-a7bf-4301-b942-345126d40ae3, 36bdaad6-61da-42d5-84cf-aaecb586068e, 315e8368-00af-469e-95cd-09210ff59434, 2a67ead4-91ec-41a1-bb8e-a47608c52152, e0e45800-cf0a-4c4a-94bf-9d50306ffe24" -->

<ol>
<li id="973aeec3-cc9f-4597-a9ed-c5db52cf0fb3" timestamp="1" place="4094d7e6-25b3-4cf1-8f95-cb20161a9756" characters="e19c3118-15cd-4a2a-b0be-62fac7918164, 36bdaad6-61da-42d5-84cf-aaecb586068e, 315e8368-00af-469e-95cd-09210ff59434" words="7040-7598">During a routine core drill by Foreman Dmitri Kolev's crew, Markus Vader's bit strikes a smooth, faintly warm black lattice that is not rock, buried far below layers that should hold nothing but stone. Bagor Vavran orders the find hidden from the Mars colonial council and the UN Space Authority so Bolo Extraction can claim sole mining rights over it, while Markus quietly pockets a sample instead of logging it, sensing it could buy his way out of Bolo for good.</li>
<li id="589e8ae4-f0cf-4567-ab11-eea32e09271d" timestamp="2" place="5a324932-47f9-4970-9866-a8240b54efaa" characters="e19c3118-15cd-4a2a-b0be-62fac7918164, 36bdaad6-61da-42d5-84cf-aaecb586068e, 48531820-f25e-452e-8b5a-c0e785daf28f, 315e8368-00af-469e-95cd-09210ff59434, e0e45800-cf0a-4c4a-94bf-9d50306ffe24, 2a67ead4-91ec-41a1-bb8e-a47608c52152, 43366964-6e56-470b-bed5-3ebec4d93645" words="407-976">The Victoria lands to find Bolo guards fencing off the ice field while settlers queue at dry water pumps. Tomas Reyes, Markus Vader's new shift lead, walks him past rigs already running hot and warns that Bagor Vavran has brought in the Victoria's crew to squeeze the colony even harder. In the council hall Bagor waves the seized claim deeds as proof that the colony drinks only what he lets them dig. Then rumours leak from Dmitri Kolev's crew that something was found, Tomas files a claim that shaft 7 lies on settler land, and Colonel Ines Haldane announces a government decree to take it for the state. Bagor makes Kolev swear before the council that nothing was found, then vows to prove the structure's value in secret and outmanoeuvre both rivals.</li>
<li id="f3d306d5-2d16-43e6-8790-6ca5f6fc4586" timestamp="3.4" place="b33b73b1-57d2-464c-9c11-c8866ce66ec9" characters="4f2073f2-a7bf-4301-b942-345126d40ae3, 36bdaad6-61da-42d5-84cf-aaecb586068e, 315e8368-00af-469e-95cd-09210ff59434, 0a75f0e7-bfb8-4b81-9230-3d058f6d9206, 95ff9cea-cc8d-49a2-af30-57c654dd37cc" words="11759-12329">Bagor secretly flies Elen in under a gagging contract to analyse scrapings Kolev cut from the structure, and when she finds living microbes inside, he forbids her to tell the UN Space Authority and orders Kolev to keep his crew sealed in the shaft while Bolo bribes council inspectors to block the colonists' and government's land claims, which so frightens the crew that they decide to talk.</li>
<li id="932762de-dfa2-40f4-9281-b762b2ad6490" timestamp="4" place="62a5909c-d4c8-4cc8-b528-547490c5044c" characters="e19c3118-15cd-4a2a-b0be-62fac7918164, 4f2073f2-a7bf-4301-b942-345126d40ae3, 315e8368-00af-469e-95cd-09210ff59434, e0e45800-cf0a-4c4a-94bf-9d50306ffe24, 2a67ead4-91ec-41a1-bb8e-a47608c52152, 36bdaad6-61da-42d5-84cf-aaecb586068e, 0a75f0e7-bfb8-4b81-9230-3d058f6d9206, 43366964-6e56-470b-bed5-3ebec4d93645" words="8089-8701">As Kolev's sealed crew cycle out of the airlock, they smuggle drill footage of the buried structure to Reyk, who broadcasts it across the colony as proof Bolo is stealing settlers' land. Meanwhile Markus Vader corners Dr Elen Markovsky, fresh from her lecture at the colony's university annex, and shows her a shard the crew pried from the structure. She dismisses it as a crystal hoax until it grows warm in her hand and pushes out a new lattice branch before her eyes, so she agrees to examine it secretly in her lab after hours, telling no one, not even her news-anchor husband. Haldane seizes on the broadcast to demand an emergency council hearing to assert the state's claim, while Bagor threatens Kolev's job and his daughter's medical payments unless he swears the footage is fake, and Kolev refuses to say anything at all.</li>
<li id="f70f492b-df47-4518-8bd0-3eef4d039803" timestamp="5" place="08db182a-6d9a-4291-9624-9a9ec46c6a9c" characters="e19c3118-15cd-4a2a-b0be-62fac7918164, f3f32f7b-5874-4559-8192-497fcf8af228, 3a8f2b8f-437f-4657-9d6c-8f10970c68fe, 4f2073f2-a7bf-4301-b942-345126d40ae3, 315e8368-00af-469e-95cd-09210ff59434, 36bdaad6-61da-42d5-84cf-aaecb586068e, 2a67ead4-91ec-41a1-bb8e-a47608c52152, e0e45800-cf0a-4c4a-94bf-9d50306ffe24, 0a75f0e7-bfb8-4b81-9230-3d058f6d9206, 43366964-6e56-470b-bed5-3ebec4d93645" words="2106-2772">When the overdriven bit shatters on the hard layer, Bagor Vavran's enforcer Dragan Petrov arrives to restart the drills before the colony's ice quota slips, so Markus Vader confronts Ines Okafor with recordings of her ice skimming until she hands over an off-books hand rig, and he carries it down the shaft and cuts through. Kolev secretly slips a core from the now-sealed shaft to Dr Elen Markovsky, newly arrived at the depot's makeshift lab to study the broadcast find, and she discovers living microbes in it, the first ever recovered. Bagor, Colonel Haldane and Tomas Reyk each corner her among the supply crates, demanding she certify the find as their own property and offering money, state authority and colonial loyalty. She resolves instead to plead for shared access at the emergency council hearing.</li>
<li id="1e17dc5e-2f5f-43fa-a2bb-30add038aa84" timestamp="5.2" place="ee33a810-48d1-4137-b8b4-124297b1bb75" characters="4f2073f2-a7bf-4301-b942-345126d40ae3, 36bdaad6-61da-42d5-84cf-aaecb586068e, 2a67ead4-91ec-41a1-bb8e-a47608c52152, e0e45800-cf0a-4c4a-94bf-9d50306ffe24, 315e8368-00af-469e-95cd-09210ff59434, 43366964-6e56-470b-bed5-3ebec4d93645, 0a75f0e7-bfb8-4b81-9230-3d058f6d9206" words="12355-12920">After Kolev's frightened crew leak word of the buried find, an emergency council hearing collapses into a fight over ownership: Bagor waves Bolo's mining charter, Haldane reads a decree seizing the artifact for the state, and Reyk declares it colony property. Elen begs them to share access because her samples show living microbes inside it, but they ignore her, and each faction leaves to send armed men to the chamber.</li>
<li id="c21799d3-52cb-437f-b175-6fee0c01aeba" timestamp="7" place="aa15d329-fac5-44de-a271-d2f8e3444aaf" characters="e19c3118-15cd-4a2a-b0be-62fac7918164, 4f2073f2-a7bf-4301-b942-345126d40ae3, 36bdaad6-61da-42d5-84cf-aaecb586068e, 2a67ead4-91ec-41a1-bb8e-a47608c52152, e0e45800-cf0a-4c4a-94bf-9d50306ffe24, 315e8368-00af-469e-95cd-09210ff59434, 0a75f0e7-bfb8-4b81-9230-3d058f6d9206, 43366964-6e56-470b-bed5-3ebec4d93645" words="9344-9968">When Elen sets the smuggled shard beside the ground scanner, it warms and pulses in step with a faint heat signal deep under the trench, revealing a much larger structure still buried there, and the three factions race for it. Bagor shuts off the colony's water pumps and seals the main lift to stall the militia, then orders Markus's crew to dig through the night despite the unsafe walls. Markus swears at him but agrees to drive the crew because he still hopes to buy his freedom. Meanwhile Colonel Haldane's troops commandeer the ore rail line and Reyk's militia blasts open a side tunnel. Kolev, ordered by Bagor to haul the drill rig down, quietly shows Elen an old ventilation shaft so she can reach the buried structure before the guns do.</li>
<li id="b4350c14-dfb5-4835-8827-475cac688f62" timestamp="8" place="7b19cf13-902a-49bc-af7d-b4a33f4e8693" characters="e19c3118-15cd-4a2a-b0be-62fac7918164, 3a8f2b8f-437f-4657-9d6c-8f10970c68fe, 48531820-f25e-452e-8b5a-c0e785daf28f, 4f2073f2-a7bf-4301-b942-345126d40ae3, 315e8368-00af-469e-95cd-09210ff59434, 36bdaad6-61da-42d5-84cf-aaecb586068e, 2a67ead4-91ec-41a1-bb8e-a47608c52152, e0e45800-cf0a-4c4a-94bf-9d50306ffe24, 43366964-6e56-470b-bed5-3ebec4d93645" words="4060-4678">Markus Vader slips past Bagor Vavran's enforcer Dragan Petrov at shift change and bores down the collapsed shaft toward trapped Tomas Reyes's beacon with the hand rig. Its core readings hit a smooth, unnatural wall buried in the ice, revealing a sealed chamber beside Tomas that makes the claim worth far more than its ice and gives Markus his leverage over Bagor. On a company radio he hears the factions bargain. Bagor offers Tomas's settler militia the colony's water back if they turn on Colonel Ines Haldane's troops. Haldane declares Bolo's charter void and promises the settlers land rights if they help seize the mine. Tomas pretends to bargain with both over his beacon link while sending his men forward. The pacts collapse within minutes as all three sides race for the chamber at once. Dmitri Kolev, hauling the drill rig behind Bolo security, warns Markus to hurry, so Markus kicks out the rusted grate of an old ventilation shaft and drops toward the artifact.</li>
<li id="963a13ed-0250-4f5c-abad-2eaee63842e8" timestamp="7.3" place="d89eca64-6910-4eab-a05b-6095983a94dd" characters="4f2073f2-a7bf-4301-b942-345126d40ae3, 36bdaad6-61da-42d5-84cf-aaecb586068e, 2a67ead4-91ec-41a1-bb8e-a47608c52152, e0e45800-cf0a-4c4a-94bf-9d50306ffe24, 315e8368-00af-469e-95cd-09210ff59434, 0a75f0e7-bfb8-4b81-9230-3d058f6d9206, 43366964-6e56-470b-bed5-3ebec4d93645" words="12944-13465">Elen slips into the chamber ahead of the armed men and records the microbes inside the artifact pulsing in response to her light, but Bolo security, Haldane's troops and Reyk's militia arrive and take up positions at the three entrances; Bagor orders Kolev to drill a core sample to stake Bolo's claim, Haldane and Reyk threaten to shoot anyone who touches it, and Elen's warning that the organisms are recoiling from the drill's vibration is shouted down as every side raises its weapons.</li>
<li id="381a00ef-2229-4af2-bafe-0e67de5dc654" timestamp="10" place="41a6f298-d99f-473e-84ff-0e5f3acfc86a" characters="e19c3118-15cd-4a2a-b0be-62fac7918164, 4f2073f2-a7bf-4301-b942-345126d40ae3, 36bdaad6-61da-42d5-84cf-aaecb586068e, 2a67ead4-91ec-41a1-bb8e-a47608c52152, e0e45800-cf0a-4c4a-94bf-9d50306ffe24, 315e8368-00af-469e-95cd-09210ff59434, 0a75f0e7-bfb8-4b81-9230-3d058f6d9206, 43366964-6e56-470b-bed5-3ebec4d93645" words="10571-11147">Markus leads the breach and finds a sealed inner core whose shell is crusted with the same mineral residue Elen has studied for years in fossil samples. She begs them to stop, warning that the lattice is guarding something living and that the microbes are withdrawing into the core. Markus has secretly recorded Bagor ordering the breach over her warning, so he blackmails Bagor into signing the contract release and steps aside, leaving Bagor to force the core open himself. Bagor reminds Kolev of his daughter's medical debt, and Kolev starts the drill. Meanwhile Haldane reads out a state seizure decree, Reyk shouts the colony's claim and Bagor waves Bolo's mining charter. As the bit bites into the shell, a militiaman fires at Kolev and wounds him, and Haldane's troops and Bolo security return fire.</li>
<li id="0cccde3c-eeaa-4fc4-a0e5-bd74ff1aadce" timestamp="11" place="610aeeb9-367b-42a0-bb54-c1cc6d40d65f" characters="e19c3118-15cd-4a2a-b0be-62fac7918164, 36bdaad6-61da-42d5-84cf-aaecb586068e, 3a8f2b8f-437f-4657-9d6c-8f10970c68fe, 4f2073f2-a7bf-4301-b942-345126d40ae3, 2a67ead4-91ec-41a1-bb8e-a47608c52152, e0e45800-cf0a-4c4a-94bf-9d50306ffe24, 315e8368-00af-469e-95cd-09210ff59434, 43366964-6e56-470b-bed5-3ebec4d93645, 0a75f0e7-bfb8-4b81-9230-3d058f6d9206" words="5817-6452">Before the alien shell the drill broke into, Bagor sends Dragan back up the shaft and offers Markus a cut of the Mars claims to keep quiet, boasting that whoever owns the drills owns the colony and that he ordered the shaft buried with the witnesses inside, unaware that the recorder under Markus's coat is capturing every word. Then gunfire erupts between Colonel Haldane's troops and Reyk's colonist militia. Dr Elen Markovsky drags the wounded Kolev behind the drill rig and sees the artifact's shell begin closing around the drill wound as its microbial signal fades, so she begs everyone to cease fire. But Haldane will not stand down unless the militia disarms first, Reyk will not unless Bolo and the troops withdraw, and Bagor orders his security to retake the drill, so the shooting spreads across the chamber.</li>
<li id="2cd21a9c-3994-47d0-85f0-658fb95e324c" timestamp="9.4" place="d89eca64-6910-4eab-a05b-6095983a94dd" characters="4f2073f2-a7bf-4301-b942-345126d40ae3, 36bdaad6-61da-42d5-84cf-aaecb586068e, 2a67ead4-91ec-41a1-bb8e-a47608c52152, e0e45800-cf0a-4c4a-94bf-9d50306ffe24, 0a75f0e7-bfb8-4b81-9230-3d058f6d9206, 43366964-6e56-470b-bed5-3ebec4d93645" words="13490-14005">While Bolo security, Colonel Haldane's government troops and Reyk's colonist militia fire on one another over who controls the chamber, the artifact seals itself and goes dark, ending the living microbial signal Elen had finally found, and Bagor is left shouting that he owns a dead stone.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: place id="7dbfbb3b-1e59-41da-a508-0c79b8dfd135" title="space" -->

<!-- cell: place id="40704c80-cd0b-41d1-9756-c31da1d3955d" title="asteroid belt" parent="7dbfbb3b-1e59-41da-a508-0c79b8dfd135" -->

<!-- cell: place id="8f9908e3-a19f-496b-aa80-d343184c8c9c" title="Victoria" parent="40704c80-cd0b-41d1-9756-c31da1d3955d" -->

<!-- cell: place id="7da064ef-7bd8-4a5d-bcab-7c332bb1e40e" title="drill bay" parent="8f9908e3-a19f-496b-aa80-d343184c8c9c" -->

<!-- cell: place id="f042f757-b7d7-44fb-96b7-73f90fd8aab9" title="mars" -->

<!-- cell: place id="319ca10b-356d-4c5c-b03d-c8cd5a34674d" title="Hellas Basin" parent="f042f757-b7d7-44fb-96b7-73f90fd8aab9" -->

<!-- cell: place id="e5cd8c57-0cc1-4f89-9f41-cb34fa1ac290" title="Bolo drill site" parent="319ca10b-356d-4c5c-b03d-c8cd5a34674d" -->

<!-- cell: place id="80dd0d18-1610-4ef2-ab62-38caeb9ac525" title="sealed chamber" parent="e5cd8c57-0cc1-4f89-9f41-cb34fa1ac290" -->

<!-- cell: place id="4094d7e6-25b3-4cf1-8f95-cb20161a9756" title="Bolo Extraction dig site 7" parent="319ca10b-356d-4c5c-b03d-c8cd5a34674d" -->

<!-- cell: place id="41a6f298-d99f-473e-84ff-0e5f3acfc86a" title="chamber beneath dig site 7" parent="319ca10b-356d-4c5c-b03d-c8cd5a34674d" -->

<!-- cell: place id="1de024f3-5c4a-45ba-8267-0468ecd06ebd" title="asteroid belt" -->

<!-- cell: place id="97692641-0990-4cb9-8ad5-f9f8c66839ff" title="Ceres" parent="1de024f3-5c4a-45ba-8267-0468ecd06ebd" -->

<!-- cell: place id="4c4222bf-2ee4-40bb-abec-435d88dad0d0" title="Bolo Extraction mining shaft 7" parent="97692641-0990-4cb9-8ad5-f9f8c66839ff" -->

<!-- cell: character id="315e8368-00af-469e-95cd-09210ff59434" title="Dmitri Kolev" concepts="abf702ed-3912-4556-846b-2e498c05652c" -->

Dmitri Kolev is a veteran Bolo Extraction drilling foreman who left the failing Lunar mines for Ceres two decades ago, and he sends most of his wages home to support his ailing daughter's medical care on Earth. That debt to the company keeps him at the drill head, and it keeps him silent when Bagor orders the strange find buried, even as his own unease grows.

<!-- cell: place id="d89eca64-6910-4eab-a05b-6095983a94dd" title="the artifact chamber" parent="97692641-0990-4cb9-8ad5-f9f8c66839ff" -->

<!-- cell: character id="2a67ead4-91ec-41a1-bb8e-a47608c52152" title="Colonel Ines Haldane" concepts="abf702ed-3912-4556-846b-2e498c05652c" -->

Colonel Ines Haldane is a career officer of the colonial administration, sent to the frontier world after Bagor's mining consortium reported the alien artifact and the colonists began claiming it as their own. She brings government troops into the chamber under orders to seize the find in the name of the state before either the corporation or Reyk's militia can, and she is unwilling to be the officer who returns empty-handed.

<!-- cell: character id="e0e45800-cf0a-4c4a-94bf-9d50306ffe24" title="Tomas Reyk" concepts="abf702ed-3912-4556-846b-2e498c05652c" -->

Tomas Reyk is a second-generation colonist and former mining foreman who organized the settlers' militia after Bolo's corporate charter and Colonel Haldane's government decrees began stripping colonists of land and water rights. He storms the chamber convinced the artifact is the colony's one chance at independence, and he is determined that neither the company nor the government will take it from the people who bled to settle the world.

<!-- cell: place id="e3c9b30c-e2ff-464e-bf3d-8069326ab207" title="ice shaft" parent="e5cd8c57-0cc1-4f89-9f41-cb34fa1ac290" -->

<!-- cell: character id="48531820-f25e-452e-8b5a-c0e785daf28f" title="Tomas Reyes" concepts="61d2ce61-6bb0-42d0-935a-855dcfb2b93c" -->

Tomas Reyes is a veteran drill operator on Markus's crew who signed a punishing contract with Bagor's mining consortium to pay for his daughter's passage off the failing colony. He keeps running the overdriven rigs because walking off the ice field would forfeit every credit he has earned.

<!-- cell: place id="abe87b51-ea89-4467-a91a-68c98e0e5313" title="earth" -->

<!-- cell: place id="11fbfcc6-5337-4b16-8829-4883caed766f" title="London" parent="abe87b51-ea89-4467-a91a-68c98e0e5313" -->

<!-- cell: place id="66faac15-0631-4282-a2c3-ce7686d33fc4" title="University of the City of London" parent="11fbfcc6-5337-4b16-8829-4883caed766f" -->

<!-- cell: place id="e9e973ca-8514-4bdf-967a-03394df17e16" title="Elen's lab" parent="66faac15-0631-4282-a2c3-ce7686d33fc4" -->

<!-- cell: place id="ee33a810-48d1-4137-b8b4-124297b1bb75" title="colonial council hall" parent="97692641-0990-4cb9-8ad5-f9f8c66839ff" -->

<!-- cell: place id="c4beee34-73ea-46be-939a-5e54a5c8f4c4" title="ice field" parent="e5cd8c57-0cc1-4f89-9f41-cb34fa1ac290" -->

<!-- cell: place id="d07dfe1a-5fd9-42d9-a74c-c59401604c94" title="scavenger ship Victoria" parent="7dbfbb3b-1e59-41da-a508-0c79b8dfd135" -->

<!-- cell: place id="521483d7-ee28-475b-8251-d5002d0e1f0e" title="Markus's bunk" parent="d07dfe1a-5fd9-42d9-a74c-c59401604c94" -->

<!-- cell: place id="b33b73b1-57d2-464c-9c11-c8866ce66ec9" title="Bolo Extraction private laboratory" parent="97692641-0990-4cb9-8ad5-f9f8c66839ff" -->

<!-- cell: place id="8e2ea47b-7b61-4323-97c9-1cc99435e238" title="collapsed ice shaft" parent="e5cd8c57-0cc1-4f89-9f41-cb34fa1ac290" -->

<!-- cell: place id="efe098dd-5ded-4ecb-9363-43ce8ede141d" title="dig site 7" parent="319ca10b-356d-4c5c-b03d-c8cd5a34674d" -->

<!-- cell: place id="a682c9e4-a6f4-4e44-81e2-ad0dba19a8c0" title="excavation trench" parent="efe098dd-5ded-4ecb-9363-43ce8ede141d" -->

<!-- cell: place id="7ee68c56-2256-45f0-923f-0cc473d93926" title="colony landing pad" parent="319ca10b-356d-4c5c-b03d-c8cd5a34674d" -->

<!-- cell: place id="f7f212f0-fa5a-4625-98d5-b38626198fdf" title="a café near the University of the City of London" parent="11fbfcc6-5337-4b16-8829-4883caed766f" -->

<!-- cell: place id="62a5909c-d4c8-4cc8-b528-547490c5044c" title="Bolo Extraction shaft airlock" parent="97692641-0990-4cb9-8ad5-f9f8c66839ff" -->

<!-- cell: place id="00d0c245-3b2f-4e78-b1a8-859113c76481" title="equipment depot" parent="e5cd8c57-0cc1-4f89-9f41-cb34fa1ac290" -->

<!-- cell: character id="f3f32f7b-5874-4559-8192-497fcf8af228" title="Ines Okafor" concepts="61d2ce61-6bb0-42d0-935a-855dcfb2b93c" -->

Ines Okafor is the depot's long-serving quartermaster, who has quietly skimmed ice from the claim's shipments for years to pay off a family debt back on the inner stations, and she keeps an off-books stash of unregistered equipment to sell along with it. When Markus confronts her with recordings of the skimming, she hands over a hand rig because exposure would cost her both her post and the money her family depends on.

<!-- cell: place id="aa15d329-fac5-44de-a271-d2f8e3444aaf" title="survey tent" parent="efe098dd-5ded-4ecb-9363-43ce8ede141d" -->

<!-- cell: place id="613e7ae4-ece1-45fe-9b3b-68425a3b2951" title="Bolo mine access tunnels" parent="97692641-0990-4cb9-8ad5-f9f8c66839ff" -->

<!-- cell: character id="3a8f2b8f-437f-4657-9d6c-8f10970c68fe" title="Dragan Petrov" concepts="61d2ce61-6bb0-42d0-935a-855dcfb2b93c" -->

Dragan Petrov is a former quarry foreman who lost his license after a cave-in killed three men under his watch, and he has worked ever since as Bagor's enforcer, keeping crews in line through intimidation. He comes to the shaft because Bagor pays off his debts and trusts him to make sure the drills restart no matter who objects.

<!-- cell: place id="08db182a-6d9a-4291-9624-9a9ec46c6a9c" title="supply depot" parent="e5cd8c57-0cc1-4f89-9f41-cb34fa1ac290" -->

<!-- cell: place id="0a4676cb-8e60-4348-b67b-f3e2c66494d6" title="Markus's rented room in Whitechapel" parent="11fbfcc6-5337-4b16-8829-4883caed766f" -->

<!-- cell: place id="648b88b7-6ba5-45d0-ba3d-b16666a61844" title="colony medical bay laboratory" parent="97692641-0990-4cb9-8ad5-f9f8c66839ff" -->

<!-- cell: place id="4aa63be1-85cd-4bf2-bb60-c23a5cf1a900" title="old ventilation shaft above the artifact chamber" parent="97692641-0990-4cb9-8ad5-f9f8c66839ff" -->

<!-- cell: place id="5a324932-47f9-4970-9866-a8240b54efaa" title="colonial council hall by the landing pad" parent="319ca10b-356d-4c5c-b03d-c8cd5a34674d" -->

<!-- cell: place id="7b19cf13-902a-49bc-af7d-b4a33f4e8693" title="collapsed ice shaft beside the sealed chamber" parent="e5cd8c57-0cc1-4f89-9f41-cb34fa1ac290" -->

<!-- cell: place id="189e3809-8263-4c30-8b23-13ae71c0d613" title="Bolo drill site (dig site 7)" parent="319ca10b-356d-4c5c-b03d-c8cd5a34674d" -->

<!-- cell: place id="77899cf1-e11a-49e2-9149-5b3da6479ead" title="collapsed ice shaft above the sealed chamber" parent="189e3809-8263-4c30-8b23-13ae71c0d613" -->

<!-- cell: place id="610aeeb9-367b-42a0-bb54-c1cc6d40d65f" title="sealed artifact chamber" parent="e5cd8c57-0cc1-4f89-9f41-cb34fa1ac290" -->

<!-- cell: place id="93413ad3-a2a5-4342-a102-afac171e1dd3" title="sealed chamber beneath dig site 7" parent="e5cd8c57-0cc1-4f89-9f41-cb34fa1ac290" -->

<!-- cell: markdown id="6d921417-f6ae-4e15-a89d-1db9832bd53c" -->

The drill bay of the *Victoria* smelled of hot iron and coolant, and the big boring head hung in its cradle like a fist that had been told to wait. Markus Vader was on his back beneath it, a wrench in one hand and a lamp clipped to his collar, when the bay's holo-emitter chimed twice and threw a column of blue light across the deck plates. Grit from the last rock still drifted in the low spin-gravity, and it sparkled where it passed through the beam.

Bagor Vavran took shape in the light, seated, his heavy hands folded on a desk that was a hundred million kilometers away. He did not wait for Markus to slide out from under the drill. "Your run is cancelled," he said. "Turn the *Victoria* for Mars. Bolo Extraction has filed on every drilling claim under the Hellas ice field and put armed crews on the rigs. That ice is the only water the colony has. Whoever holds those claims holds the colony, and I don't intend to let it be Bolo."

Markus sat up and wiped his palms on his coveralls, leaving two dark streaks. "This is an ore hauler, Mr. Vavran. Half my crew has never set foot on a planet. You want people to stand up to armed crews, you hire people who do that for a living."

"I hired you." Bagor's image flickered as the signal lag caught up, then steadied. "Seven years remain on your contract, Mr. Vader. Read the clause on reassignment if you've forgotten it. The ship is mine, the drill is mine, and until that contract is paid out, your time is mine. Course corrections are already in your navigation queue." The light folded in on itself and was gone, and the bay was dark again except for the small lamp at Markus's collar.

He stayed where he was for a while, looking at the empty patch of air. Then he got to his feet, set the wrench back in its rack, and crossed to the bay's comm panel. The queued corrections were waiting on the screen, already signed with Bagor's code. Markus pressed his thumb to the panel to confirm them, and somewhere far aft the *Victoria*'s engines began to turn her slowly away from the rocks.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="623b6254-73c4-49a4-a8a8-ea454907de2c">The *Victoria* came down on the pad at Hellas Basin, and Markus followed the colonists toward the council hall beside it.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="003ad7d9-be46-4a86-be06-7d490ae64f49" -->

The *Victoria* came down on the Hellas pad in a storm of red grit. When the dust thinned, Markus Vader saw the first thing the colony had to show him: a line of Bolo guards in matte armour, stringing razor fence along the edge of the ice field while their rifles hung easy across their chests. Beyond them, settlers stood in a queue that bent twice around the pump house. Most held empty jerricans. The pumps at its head coughed, shuddered, and gave nothing.

Tomas Reyes met him at the foot of the ramp. He was a lean man with frost-burn across both cheeks, and he introduced himself as Markus's new shift lead without offering a hand. He walked Markus past the drilling rigs. Every one of them ran hot, and their housings ticked and smoked in the thin cold air. "They're pushing these past tolerance," Tomas said, low. "Vavran wants every metre of ice cut before the season turns. And the crew you came down with? That's his doing. He paid their passage. More hands to squeeze us with." He glanced back at the *Victoria*, where men in Bolo grey were already unloading crates. "Watch who you drink with."

The council hall stood a hundred metres from the pad. It was a pressurised dome of patched polymer, and it smelled of recycled breath. Bagor Vavran stood at the head table with a sheaf of claim deeds in his fist. He waved them over the benches like a priest with a relic. "Every bore in this basin is mine," he said. "Every metre of ice. You drink what I let you dig, and you dig where I tell you." Nobody answered him. Near the back, a young driller named Tomas Reyk leaned toward his neighbour and whispered. The whisper passed down the bench to the next one and the next. Dmitri Kolev's crew had hit something in shaft 7. It was not ice and not rock. It was something built.

The hall did not stay quiet after that. Tomas Reyes got to his feet with a datapad and announced, loud enough to reach the doors, that he was filing a claim. Shaft 7, he said, lay on settler land under the original charter survey. Before Bagor could answer, Colonel Ines Haldane rose from the side gallery in her grey government coat. She did not raise her voice. By decree of the Colonial Authority, she said, any anomalous find in the Hellas Basin was property of the state, and shaft 7 was sealed as of that moment. Bagor's face went still. He turned to the third bench and pointed. "Kolev. Stand up." Dmitri Kolev stood. He was a heavy man, and his hands were still grey with drill slurry. "Swear it, here, before the council. Did your crew find anything in that shaft?" Kolev looked at the floor, then at Haldane, then at Bagor's guards along the wall. "Nothing," he said. "We found nothing."

Bagor smiled and let the deeds fall to the table. As the benches emptied, he stayed behind with one of his Bolo officers and spoke too quietly for the room to hear. Only the officer's nod showed. Then Bagor said the last part aloud, flat and certain, to the empty chairs where Haldane and Reyes had sat: "Let them squabble over paper. I'll know what's down there before either of them gets a shovel in the ground."

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="ad1b099b-2838-4c65-9f79-1ba4145782c5">Markus Vader followed Tomas Reyes out across the ice field to the Bolo drill site.</li>
<li edge="6e1c7df8-3fe8-4b88-b415-44bf534ac348">Bagor Vavran left the council hall for Bolo Extraction's private laboratory on Ceres.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: character id="43366964-6e56-470b-bed5-3ebec4d93645" title="Ines Haldane" -->

Colonel Ines Haldane is the Colonial Authority's resident liaison in the Hellas Basin, a career officer posted there quietly two years ago after an orbital survey flagged readings beneath the ice that nobody could explain. Her standing orders are to secure anything artificial the drillers turn up before a private claimant like Bagor Vavran can bury it or sell it, so the whisper about shaft 7 is the moment she has been waiting for.

<!-- cell: markdown id="b2bb9dfa-e727-4b66-ad93-a889a93fec6c" -->

The Victoria's crew came down onto the Hellas ice field in the long copper dusk, and before their boots had dried the hab airlock Bagor Vavran had posted the new numbers on every screen at the Bolo drill site. Extraction quota tripled. Colony water ration cut by half, effective at once. Anything the settlers needed beyond the ration they could buy from the company tanks at Bolo prices, which meant they would be paying for ice they had cut from the ground with their own hands. Vavran read the figures aloud in the mess hall without raising his voice, and nobody answered him, because the only door out of the basin was a ship he owned.

Markus Vader went looking for the site's medic afterward and found Tomas Reyes in the infirmary, taping a cracked visor seal under a work lamp. Reyes did not need to be asked. He pulled up Markus's contract and turned the screen around. Walk off before term, it said, and every credit banked toward his daughter's passage reverted to Bolo. Reyes tapped the clause twice and said nothing more, and Markus stood there long enough for the visor seal to set. That night he took the rigs off their governors. The drills screamed in the thin air, their housings frosting and glowing at once, and he sank the shaft past the posted limit, then past the depth where the old survey charts simply stopped, while the core samples came up darker and wetter and strangely warm.

Near the bottom of the last run the bit brought up something that was not ice. It was a shard no longer than a finger, grey-green and faintly translucent, with edges too clean to have broken that way. Markus slipped it into his glove before the logger could tag it and carried it back to his bunk wrapped in a sock. By morning it had changed. From one face a lattice had grown, a branching filament finer than hair, each joint repeating the last in perfect miniature. He was holding it up to the bunk light when Reyes put his head through the curtain to check his frostbitten knuckles, stopped, and looked for a long time. "That's not rock," Reyes said quietly. "That's not ours."

Markus paid him there in the cramped bunk, a transfer that cost him three weeks of the quota bonus Vavran had dangled over the crew, and Reyes took it without argument and pulled the curtain shut behind him. When the Victoria loaded for the flight home, Markus packed the shard inside a coolant canister among his rig spares, and the Bolo cargo scan swept over it and passed it as frozen sealant. Strapped into his couch with the canister under his bunk, he thought of the only name he knew that belonged to someone who studied traces of life from beyond the Earth: Dr Elen Markovsky, in London. He would find her, he decided, and he would put the thing into her hands.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="2219a989-ceb8-4b0c-b363-d9721225724f">Back at the Hellas ice shaft, Markus stood over the overdriven rig as the bit sank toward the bottom of the bore.</li>
<li edge="ff15a2ed-fe2c-4676-ad3a-9ad54840b9bc">On Ceres, Markus waited outside the Bolo shaft airlock as Dr Elen Markovsky came from her lecture.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: character id="0a75f0e7-bfb8-4b81-9230-3d058f6d9206" title="Elen Markovsky" -->

Dr Elen Markovsky is a London astrobiologist whose decades of searching for traces of life beyond Earth won her a famous name but no proof, after Bolo quietly buried the anomalous samples she once requested from its Martian drill sites. When a stranger arrives with a grey-green shard that grows, she takes it as the evidence she has waited her whole career for and the leverage to make Bolo answer for what it has hidden.

<!-- cell: markdown id="50315908-88f0-43f1-90e7-52530d90e040" -->

The shaft had been cut to four hundred meters, and the bit was turning at six hundred and twelve. The safety limit was stenciled in yellow on the rim of the drill housing, half buried under a crust of frost that no one had bothered to scrape. Down here the ice of the Hellas Basin was old and blue-black, striated with the dust of storms that had blown before anything on Earth had learned to crawl. The work lamps threw the crew's shadows long against the curving walls, and every breath hung in the air a moment before the scrubbers pulled it away.

The sound changed first. For hours the bit had chewed through ice with a wet, crunching rhythm, steady as a heartbeat. Then it began to scream. The whole rig shuddered on its anchors, and the readout on Tomas Reyes's wrist flickered red: torque spiking, bit temperature climbing, penetration rate falling to nothing. He killed the feed and leaned over the bore. The meltwater at the bottom had drained away, and beneath it lay a surface as smooth and dark as poured glass. The diamond teeth had barely scored it. Tomas wiped the frost from his visor and turned to Markus Vader. "That's not basalt," he said. "Nothing out here polishes like that. We should stop and send for a survey team."

The holo-link on the rig's control post bloomed blue before Markus could answer. Bagor Vavran's face hung in the cold air, rendered a little too large, his collar crisp, a window of warm amber light behind him three hundred kilometers away. He had been listening on the open channel. "Nobody is sending for anyone," he said. "Bolo is eleven days behind quota. If that bit isn't through by the end of shift, Mr. Reyes, your contract is void, and so are the rest of yours. And I'll be forced to review the colony's water allocation. Hellas Station drinks what this shaft produces. I'm sure none of you want to explain to the families why the tanks ran dry." He smiled as if he had offered them a favor.

Markus said nothing for a long moment. He looked at the black surface at the bottom of the bore, then at Tomas, whose jaw was set hard behind his visor. Then he turned his back on the holo, reached into the inner lining of his coat, and slid a thumb-sized recorder from a seam he had opened weeks ago. He clipped its lead to the comm wire at his collar, pressed it until the tiny diode glowed green, and smoothed the lining flat over it. When he faced the projection again his voice was level and loud enough to carry. "Let me be sure I have it right, Mr. Vavran. You're ordering us past the safety limit, into an unidentified formation, under threat of cutting the station's water."

"I'm ordering you to do your job," Bagor said.

"Understood." Markus pulled his gloves tight and nodded to the crew. "Bring it back up to speed. Bore on." The bit spun up, the scream returned, and far below them the dark stone took it without a mark.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="8fb0e205-5f95-4930-a7e7-3ef0a3bf3483">Markus Vader kept the overdriven bit screaming against the black layer while its telemetry ran up the line to the supply depot.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="e71f43e2-9f95-4678-b3bd-89af6a76c734" -->

The bit shattered at four hundred and twelve meters with a sound the depot crew felt in their teeth before they heard it. Telemetry flatlined, and the overdriven motor whined on against nothing until someone killed the feed. Within the hour Dragan Petrov came through the airlock in a pressure suit stenciled with Bagor Vavran's crest. He did not take off his helmet. He stood among the stacked fuel cells and spare casings and told the drill crew that the colony's ice quota closed at the end of the sol, that Vavran did not intend to explain a shortfall to anyone, and that the drills would be turning again before he left the depot.

Markus Vader did not argue with him. He went instead to the back of the depot, where Ines Okafor kept her inventory terminal, and set a datapad on the console between them. It held weeks of camera logs: ice hauled off the scales at night, crates relabeled as regolith, a hand rig carried in and out through the maintenance lock. Okafor watched the footage loop twice without speaking. Then she unlocked a crate marked *filtration media* and lifted out the rig, a squat diamond-toothed corer with no registry number on its housing. Vader slung it over his shoulder and rode the cage down the shaft alone. The crew topside listened to the comm channel crackle with his breathing and the grind of the corer against the hard layer for most of an hour, until a dull crack came up the line and he reported he was through. The shaft was sealed behind him by order before he had his helmet off.

Dmitri Kolev had been on the winch. When the last core came up he did not log it. He wrapped it in a thermal sleeve, carried it past the sealed hatch, and set it on the folding table that Dr Elen Markovsky had turned into a laboratory between two pallets of oxygen candles. She had come to the depot to study the find that had already been broadcast across the colony, and she had barely unpacked. Kolev left without a word. Markovsky thawed a sliver of the core under the scope, and in the meltwater something moved. She ran the stain, ran it again, and watched the cells take up the dye and divide. They were alive, the first living microbes ever recovered from Martian ground, and she had held the core for less than a day.

The visitors came to her one at a time, in the narrow aisles between the supply crates, as though each believed the others had not yet heard. Bagor Vavran spoke softly and named a figure large enough to buy her a ship home, and asked only that her certification list the organisms as recovered on Vavran claim and therefore Vavran property. Colonel Ines Haldane did not offer money. She cited the emergency statutes and the chain of authority, and told Markovsky that a discovery of this weight belonged to the state that had carried every colonist here. Tomas Reyk came last. He was dusty from the drill floor, and he leaned against a crate of ration bricks and reminded her who had dug the shaft and who had breathed this air for years before she arrived. He said the find belonged to the colony and to no one standing outside it.

Markovsky thanked each of them and promised none of them anything. When the depot lights dimmed for night cycle she sat alone at the folding table with the scope still lit and the cells still dividing under it. On the back of a cargo manifest she drafted what she would say at the emergency council hearing. The organisms would not be certified as anyone's property. Every party would have access to the samples, the shaft, and the data, under shared terms. She read it over once, folded the manifest into her pocket, and turned the scope light down to keep from waking the depot.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="4f9c64a4-f6c1-422c-9c59-e86adbc09f90">Markus Vader went out to the Bolo drill site in Hellas Basin, where his crew was driving the drills into the colony's ice field.</li>
<li edge="b14e6d06-92e9-4a00-9af4-7da5db1933b0">Elen Markovsky carried her folded manifest to the emergency council hearing in the colonial council hall on Ceres.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="f48333e2-aec3-4f7d-b905-184a4afcf76a" -->

The Bolo rig stood at the bottom of Hellas Basin under a sky the color of weak tea. Its three drill towers groaned in the thin wind, and the pressure gauges on every head had sat in the red since the shift began. Markus Vader had ordered them held there. The colony's ice field ran beneath the site in a pale seam forty meters thick, and Bagor Vavran wanted all of it stripped and sold before the settlement council finished arguing over who owned it. At sixty-two meters the number-two bit shrieked, bucked, and dropped into nothing. The return feed coughed up no ice. It brought up black grit, a breath of air warmer than anything on Mars had a right to be, and then silence.

Tomas Reyes went down the shaft on a cable with a lamp, and his helmet camera carried the picture up to the control hut. Beneath the ice lay a chamber with walls too straight and corners too exact to be the work of any flood or fault. A lattice of dark, glassy struts crossed it in repeating hexagons, every join smooth as a weld, and the whole structure gave off a faint heat that fogged the edge of Tomas's visor. Behind Markus the holo-link flickered, and Bagor's face hung there in blue light, watching. He did not ask what it was. He asked who else had seen it. When Markus gave him the names, Tomas and the two men on the winch and the tech at the feed, Bagor told him to blow the shaft charges and bring the ceiling down on all of it, workers included. *A cave-in*, he said. *Drilling past limits. Tragic. Nobody's claim gets frozen over a hole in the ground.*

Markus said yes. While he said it, his thumb rested on the record stud of his wrist unit, which had been running since the bit broke through. It held Bagor's face, Bagor's voice, and the chamber glowing on the monitor behind them. When the link went dark he took the lift cage down himself, past Tomas, who was still calling up for orders. At the bottom he snapped a finger-length shard from the nearest strut, pushed it into his thigh pocket, and climbed. He did not look back down the shaft. He crossed the dust flats to the prefab dome of Dr Elen Markovsky's site lab with his breath loud in his helmet and the shard warm against his leg through the suit.

Elen did not ask questions until the shard was under her scope, and after that she stopped asking. The lattice was sealed, she said. It was not grown and not cracked from any rock, but closed on itself like a vessel. Inside its glassy skin lay layer on layer of mineral banding, the iron-and-sulfur signature that turned up in old Martian sediment everywhere and that everyone credited to single-celled organisms nobody had ever found. Those organisms had never been found because they had never been there, not in the open. They had been inside this thing. Markus copied her readout beside his recording and opened the holo-link again.

He kept his terms short. He wanted his contract voided, a berth on the next orbital shuttle, and his name gone from the Bolo payroll. If he got them, the recording stayed in his pocket. Bagor listened without blinking, and when Markus finished he looked past him to the scope, to the banded shard, and to the faint shimmer of heat still rising from it in the cold air of the lab. Something moved behind Bagor's eyes, and it was not fear. *Warm*, he said. *After how long in the ice? That is no fossil. That is a power source.* Twenty minutes later his security crawler pulled up outside the dome with its lights burning. Markus and Elen were put into the lift cage with a crate of cutting gear between them and lowered back down toward the dark at sixty-two meters, where the rest of the lattice was waiting.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="1d973490-288b-4084-bde4-2d65c4d665aa">Markus fixed his eyes on the rim wall above the shaft, where the equipment depot and its comm bench still listened to the basin.</li>
<li edge="59874025-9f0b-47db-8503-1517658a50a5">Elen kept the warm shard close and thought of the ground scanner waiting in the survey tent at dig site 7.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="32e0f8ba-5f68-4ae6-8a63-7f6eb7ef52f3" -->

The equipment depot at Bolo sat half-buried against the rim wall, a corrugated hangar pressurized to the thin edge of comfort, its racks lit amber by work lamps that never fully warmed. Dust from the collapse outside still sifted through the outer seals and settled on everything in a fine ochre skin. Markus Vader stood at the comm bench with his helmet off and a receiver pressed flat to his ear, and he had not moved in some time. Beneath the hiss of the basin's static there was a pulse, faint and regular, three short and a pause: a suit beacon on its last cells. The tag that crawled across the display read REYES, T.

He crossed to the drill bay. Six heavy rigs stood in their cradles, and every one of them showed the same red plate on its control face: LOCKOUT, AUTHORIZATION VAVRAN, B. He tried his own codes on the nearest, then on the next, and the plates did not change. On the depot's wall screen Bagor Vavran's face appeared, already looking past him at something offscreen. "The claim is unstable," Bagor said. "Nobody cuts until survey clears it. That's the end of it." The screen went dark before Markus could answer, and the beacon kept ticking in his ear.

Ines Okafor was at the quartermaster's cage, logging filter canisters with a stylus. She did not look up when he leaned on the counter, and she did not look up when he set his slate down in front of her, open to a column of figures: ice tonnage out of the Bolo cores against tonnage received at the Hellas refinery, month by month, and the steady, careful gap between them. Then she looked up. "There's a hand rig," Markus said quietly. "The one that never made it onto the manifest. I want it, and a full cell pack, and I want it now." Her jaw worked. For a long moment the only sound was the hum of the scrubbers. Then she unlocked the back of the cage, dragged out a scuffed case with no serial stencil on it, and shoved it across the floor to his boots without a word.

Before he hefted it, Markus sat down at the comm bench one more time. He bundled the ledger, the recording of Bagor's refusal, and a log of the beacon's pulse into a single packet, split it three ways, and routed the copies to relays outside the depot's network, one to the refinery, one to the orbital registry, and one to an address he did not say aloud. He set a timer to release them unless he reset it every twelve hours. Ines watched him do it from behind the cage. When he was finished he sealed his helmet, lifted the hand rig onto his shoulder, and walked toward the airlock, where the beacon was still counting out its three short pulses and a pause.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="91db1fe3-d05f-465c-84b7-b810aa6f59b7">Markus shouldered the hand rig out of the airlock and across the basin to the Bolo drill site, following Tomas Reyes's beacon to the collapsed ice shaft.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: character id="19d4d1f6-b096-4ed8-a6b3-7d3ca629ea7c" title="T. Reyes" -->

Teodora Reyes is a veteran core driller on the Bolo ice claim who was working a deep section of the rim when it collapsed, and she is now trapped beneath the rubble with her suit beacon on its last cells. She had been sent into that unstable section on Bagor Vavran's orders to keep the core tonnage flowing, so the lockout that keeps anyone from cutting her free also keeps anyone from looking too closely at the work she was doing down there.

<!-- cell: markdown id="590811be-a182-420e-bba2-7461328abe84" -->

Shift change at the Bolo drill site came with a shudder of floodlights as the generators cycled, and in that half-second of dark Markus Vader slipped past Dragan Petrov. Bagor Vavran's enforcer stood at the lip of the collapsed ice shaft with his rifle slung and his helmet turned toward the relief crawler grinding down the ramp, and he never saw the hand rig go over the edge on its tether. Markus followed it down. The shaft had folded in on itself when the lower gallery gave way, leaving a slanted throat of fractured ice and regolith that glittered faintly blue in his lamp. Somewhere below, Tomas Reyes's beacon pulsed on his visor, a green tick every four seconds, steady as a heartbeat.

He braced his boots against the wall and set the hand rig to bore. The bit chewed through dirty Hellas ice in a spray of frost that hung in the thin air before drifting down past him like slow snow. The core readings scrolled up his wrist display: water ice, perchlorate salts, dust bands from storms a million years old. Then the numbers flattened. The bit skated, whined, and stopped dead against something it could not cut. Markus pulled the core and turned it in his gloved hand. The end of it was polished. Not fractured, not ground, but smooth as poured glass. He moved the rig a meter to the left and bored again, then a meter lower, and each time the same wall answered him, buried in the ice and running beside the beacon's position in a curve too regular to be stone. Behind it the sonar showed nothing at all, a sealed hollow. The water in this basin had been worth killing over. This was worth more than all of it, and as Markus stood there with the glassy core in his fist he understood that whoever held the chamber would hold Bagor Vavran by the throat.

His company radio crackled with voices bleeding over one another on the open band. Bagor's came first, warm and reasonable: the colony's water rights restored in full, every reservoir unlocked, if Tomas's settler militia would turn their guns on Colonel Haldane's troops. Before he had finished, Colonel Ines Haldane cut across him, clipped and formal, declaring Bolo's charter void under emergency authority and promising the settlers title to the land itself if they helped seize the mine. Then Tomas, his voice thin and hoarse through the beacon link, answered each of them in turn, asking about terms, about guarantees, about timelines, stretching every sentence. Underneath the talk, on the settlers' own frequency, Markus caught the short bursts of a different conversation: move up, second team left, go. Tomas was bargaining with both of them and sending his men forward the whole time.

It lasted minutes. Someone on Haldane's channel reported settler movement on the east ramp; Bagor's men reported Haldane's troops cutting the perimeter fence; the polite offers dissolved into shouted orders, and all three sides were running for the chamber at once. Dmitri Kolev's voice broke in on the crew band, ragged with effort, the drone of the drill rig's treads behind him as he hauled it in the wake of Bolo security. "Markus, they're coming down. All of them. Whatever you found, get to it now." Markus swept his lamp along the shaft wall until it caught a square of corroded metal half swallowed by ice: the grate of an old ventilation shaft, its bolts bled orange with rust. He drove his boot into it once, twice. On the third kick it tore loose and tumbled into the black, and Markus dropped after it, down toward the smooth wall and whatever waited behind it.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="12a76dbf-311b-4773-9583-1dd856a28a07">Markus climbed back out for Dr Elen Markovsky and a hand rig, and led her down into the collapsed shaft at dig site 7.</li>
<li edge="4b342c6a-9430-46ac-89fa-490c9ce2f45c">Far out on Ceres, Dr Elen Markovsky slipped alone into the artifact chamber, a step ahead of the armed men.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="406a9a82-b4b8-469f-a11d-1ae674fa4319" -->

The shaft at dig site 7 had folded in on itself, a throat of shattered ice and red rubble sloping down into the dark beneath the Hellas Basin. Markus Vader went first, the hand rig braced against his hip, its cutting head still stenciled with another crew's serial number. Dr Elen Markovsky followed with the lamp and the recorder. The rig shrieked through frozen regolith and sheared ice into a glittering grit that hung in their helmet beams. They cut for most of an hour, shoring the gap with salvaged struts. Then the last slab gave way, and a breath of trapped air puffed outward. Behind it, in a pocket no larger than a supply locker, Tomas Reyes lifted one gloved hand, his suit gauge blinking amber but not red.

Beyond him the pocket opened onto a wall that was not ice. It was smooth and seamless, and it sealed a chamber they could see into only through a hairline fracture. Inside, the artifact filled the space: a vast lattice of interlocking struts, dark as basalt and veined with a dull inner glow, older than any record humans had ever kept. Frost would not settle on it. Elen held her thermal reader to the crack and watched the numbers climb, fall, and climb again in a slow, patient rhythm. "That isn't discharge," she said quietly, still filming. "A battery doesn't breathe. That's something being kept warm. Something being kept alive."

Markus crouched beside Tomas, checked his seals, and then thumbed through the footage on his wrist unit. He cut a clip of the lattice, the heat curve, and the sealed wall, and sent it up the relay to Bagor Vavran with a single line: *Full recordings are on a dead-man's switch to the colony council. Come down and see what you own.* He did not wait for a reply.

Bagor came down the cut shaft in a pressure suit too clean for the work, two security men at his back, and stood at the fracture for a long moment with the lattice's glow on his faceplate. Elen started to explain the thermal cycle, and he talked over her before she reached the second sentence. "Heat is heat, Doctor. Heat is power. I didn't sink a fortune into this hole to tiptoe around it." He turned to his men. "Breach it. Now."

Markus spoke quietly, before the security men could reach the rig. "They'll crack the wall wrong and bring the shaft down on all of you. I know how this ice moves." He held Bagor's gaze through two layers of glass. "I lead the breach. In return I get the mineral claims on the whole sector, signed over, and my contract released, signed and filed before the cutter touches that wall." Behind him Elen went still, and Tomas, propped against the rubble, said nothing at all. Inside the chamber the lattice brightened, dimmed, and brightened again, as steady as a heartbeat.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="e0d33cd8-a7f0-4988-9abc-f8d8c0fb50c0">Markus hauled Tomas back up the collapsed shaft toward the rigs waiting at the surface.</li>
<li edge="d99a770c-4b3e-4830-aa2d-31dbe6a038ba">Markus took the rig to the sealed wall to lead the breach into the chamber.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="22b67b9f-fde6-41bc-839d-55926758ecb5" -->

The shaft had folded in on itself sometime before dawn, and by the time the crawler from the main camp ground to a halt at its lip, the Hellas morning had turned the frost on the spoil heaps a dull rust-pink. Bagor Vavran climbed down first, slow and heavy in a suit cut to his own measure, the company sigil stenciled across its chestplate in gold that had long since sandblasted to brown. Dragan Petrov came after him and stood a pace behind and to the left, where he always stood, one gloved hand resting on the stubby cutting tool clipped at his hip. The crew had gathered at a careful distance from the collapse, a dozen suits in the thin pinkish light, and none of them came forward to greet him.

Bagor looked at the slumped ring of regolith, at the ice glinting where the walls had sheared, and at the three drill rigs parked at the rim with their governors stripped and their warning lights blinking amber. "Start them," he said over the open channel. "All three. Full overdrive. I want this hole packed to the surface by noon and nobody digging in it again for a hundred years." Nobody moved, and Dragan turned his visor slowly across the crew, one face at a time.

Then the winch cable at the shaft's edge jerked. It drew tight and began to creak upward, and the crew pressed in despite themselves as a shape rose out of the dark: two suits lashed together, one hauling and one hanging slack. Markus Vader dragged himself over the lip on his elbows and rolled clear. He pulled Tomas Reyes up after him, and Tomas's helmet was cracked in a white starburst across the left side, but his chest pack showed green and his boots were kicking weakly against the frost. Someone shouted Tomas's name, and then three of them were on their knees beside him, slapping sealant over the crack. Markus got to his feet without help. He held up his right hand so every visor on the rim could see it, and the light in his palm showed red. His fist was clenched around a grip trigger, with a thin cable running from it back down into the shaft.

"It's live," Markus said, his voice rasping across the channel. "My grip goes slack, and everything down in that chamber goes with it. Run your drills, Vavran. Shake the ground hard enough and my hand might slip." Dragan's hand closed on the cutter, but Bagor lifted two fingers without looking back, and the enforcer went still. For a long moment the only sound on the channel was Tomas's ragged breathing and the tick of the idling rigs.

"Shut them down," Bagor said at last. The amber lights winked out one by one. He walked to the edge of the shaft, peered at the cable vanishing into the ice, and then turned to Markus with something that was almost a smile behind his faceplate. "Show me, then. Show me what you think is worth all this." Markus clipped himself back onto the winch line and went over the edge. Bagor followed him down into the dark, with Dragan close behind as always.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="7e280215-0218-4dab-927c-a550ec13ebef">Markus led Bagor and Dragan down the winch line, following the cable into the chamber at the bottom of the shaft.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="a1cb2a3d-d2f4-4c72-a266-afc492747238" -->

The chamber lay at the bottom of the Bolo shaft like a held breath, its walls of fused basalt glazed black by the cutting heads. At its center rose the shell: a curved, pearl-grey mass the size of a house, ridged like the inside of a seashell, with the drill bit still buried in a ragged wound along its flank. Work lamps hung from the rig's gantry and threw long shadows across the floor, and in the thin, chilled air everyone's breath showed white inside their faceplates. Bagor Vavran stood with one gloved hand resting on the drill housing as though it were the flank of a prize animal. He did not look at Dragan Petrov when he spoke. "Go up. Check the lift. Don't come back down until I call." Dragan hesitated, glanced once at Markus Vader, then turned and climbed into the cage, and the cable hummed him away into the dark.

When the hum had faded, Bagor stepped closer to Markus. "You're a practical man, so I'll speak plainly. Three percent of the Hellas claims. Water rights, mineral rights, all of it, in your name. You saw nothing down here but a rock formation." He smiled behind the visor. "Understand how this planet works. Whoever owns the drills owns the colony. Air, water, heat, it all comes up a pipe I paid for. The Kasei shaft? I had it buried, and the men who saw what it hit are still in it. Nobody misses a survey crew." Markus said nothing. Under the heavy fold of his coat a small recorder sat against his ribs, its indicator dark and its memory filling with every word.

The first shot came from the far side of the chamber, a flat crack that the thin air swallowed almost at once. Then came another, and then the stutter of rifles as Colonel Ines Haldane's troops, in their grey armor, answered from behind a stack of casing pipe. Tomas Reyk's colonists returned fire from the rubble at the shaft mouth, a ragged militia in patched pressure suits and mismatched helmets. Dmitri Kolev went down in the open between the two lines, clutching his thigh, and Dr Elen Markovsky ran out to him without a weapon. She caught him under the arms and hauled him backward, heels scraping the glassy floor, until they were both wedged behind the drill rig's treads. Kolev's suit hissed where the round had gone through, and she slapped a patch over it with shaking hands. Then she looked up at the shell. The wound where the bit had entered was closing. Grey material crept inward around the steel like a slow tide, sealing the breach, and on the scanner strapped to her wrist the faint microbial signal she had been tracking for hours flickered, thinned, and began to drop away.

"Stop!" Elen shouted, and she keyed her suit radio to every open channel. "Stop shooting! It's alive. It's dying, or it's shutting us out. Whatever you're fighting over is going to be gone in minutes. Cease fire, all of you!" Haldane's voice came back clipped and level: her troops would hold position until the militia laid down its arms, and not one second before. Reyk answered over her that his people would disarm the day Bolo and the army were back on the surface and off the claims, and not before. Then Bagor's voice cut across both of them, cold and unhurried, ordering his security team to retake the drill. Bolo's guards broke from cover toward the rig, and both sides turned their fire on them and on each other. Muzzle flashes stitched the dark from wall to wall, rounds sparked off the gantry above Elen's head, and behind her the shell went on closing, silent and patient, around the steel that had wounded it.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="d08f377d-6f88-4b22-a778-fd10ad65a35e">Markus Vader turned to face Bagor Vavran beside the shell, the recorder still running under his coat.</li>
<li edge="015c05a3-e1b7-4027-b341-2350052c1b58">Elen Markovsky stayed pressed behind the rig's treads, her eyes moving between the closing shell and the scanner on her wrist.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="1f881fd8-222f-4d26-820b-ff2cc876f51e" -->

The chamber lay forty meters beneath dig site 7, where the Bolo drill had punched through basalt into a hollow no survey had predicted. Its walls were too smooth to be natural, a seamless gray curve sweating frost under the work lamps, and at its center stood the thing the miners had whispered about for a week: a ribbed ovoid the height of a man, half-sunk in red dust, cold as the deep rock around it. Markus Vader came down the ladder last, his suit lamp swinging, a data slate clipped to his chest. Bagor Vavran was already there, a pry bar in one gloved hand, with Dr. Elen Markovsky standing a careful distance behind him, her sample case pressed to her side.

"I have the recordings," Markus said over the open channel. "The chamber, the survey logs, and you, Bagor, ordering the shaft collapsed with Teodor's crew still inside it because they'd seen too much. It's all copied, all timestamped." Bagor didn't turn around. He fitted the bar into the seam along the ovoid's flank and leaned his weight on it, grunting, and said only that a power source like this would buy him the whole Hellas Basin and that no miner's tape would matter once the buyers on Earth saw it lit. Markovsky told him to stop. The seam cracked anyway with a sound like ice breaking on a lake.

There was no light, no surge of energy. A thin breath of gas fogged out of the split shell, and inside lay hundreds of small sealed cells packed in a lattice like a honeycomb, many of them already sweating moisture as the warmth of the lamps reached them. Fine dark filaments threaded from the broken cells into the dust at the ovoid's base and began, slowly and visibly, to spread. Markovsky knelt before anyone could stop her, held a sampler to the stain, and read the scope with her breath gone ragged. "Single-celled," she said. "Chemolithotrophs. These are the makers. The isotope ratios in the Noachian clays, the traces we've chased for sixty years and never found the bodies for. They didn't die out. They packed themselves away." She looked up at the ribbed shell. "It isn't a battery, Vavran. It's a seed vault, and you've just planted it."

Bagor stood over the spreading stain with the pry bar hanging from his fist. A living biosphere could not be sold, mined, or fenced; within the hour the colony's own law would freeze every claim in the basin as a protected biological site. Markus unclipped the slate and held it up so the lamp caught its screen. "You can't own this," he said. "But you can still go to prison for Teodor. So here's your choice." Bagor tried bluster, then bargaining, then silence, and in the end he signed on Markus's slate with a stiff glove: every Mars claim held by Vavran Extraction transferred to the colony council, and the indenture contract binding Markus Vader to the drills cancelled in full. Markovsky witnessed both signatures. Then Markus climbed the ladder out of the chamber, past the idle Bolo rig, and walked across the dust toward the colony domes without looking back, the first morning in nine years that no one owned his hours.

<!-- cell: character id="86dc897a-e3c2-4b0b-8730-00395393827d" title="Teodor" -->

Teodor was a veteran shift foreman with Vavran Extraction who led the first crew to break into the hidden chamber beneath dig site 7, and Bagor Vavran had the shaft collapsed on him and his men to keep their discovery quiet. Though dead before the scene begins, he takes part through Markus Vader, his friend and fellow indentured miner, who carries the recordings of Teodor's murder down the ladder as the leverage that finally breaks Bagor.

<!-- cell: markdown id="ba1f90e3-ad30-49fd-aca8-34c288d96623" -->

The drill rig at dig site 7 stood on the floor of the Hellas Basin like a heron in a dry lake, its derrick lit amber against a sky gone the color of weak tea. Down here, seven kilometers below the planet's mean datum, the air was thick enough to hold a haze of fine dust that never quite settled, and the crew moved through it in pressure suits stenciled with the blue-and-white chevron of Bolo Extraction. Foreman Dmitri Kolev stood at the console with his visor tilted toward the depth readout, watching the numbers climb through basalt and ancient sediment, through four hundred meters of nothing but stone. Beside the bore, Markus Vader rode the feed controls with one gloved hand, the way he had on a thousand routine cores before.

At four hundred and twelve meters the rig changed its voice. The grinding whine dropped to a thin, high keen, and the torque gauge spiked, fell, and spiked again. Kolev called the halt. When the bit came up, its diamond teeth were polished flat, and caught in the core barrel was a fragment of something black and smooth, a lattice of thin interlocking struts that branched and rejoined in a pattern too regular for crystal and too intricate for anything poured. Markus turned it under the work lamp. Through the fingertips of his glove he felt it, or thought he did: a faint warmth, steady as a held breath, coming from a thing that had lain beneath half a kilometer of frozen rock since before the basin had a name.

Bagor Vavran arrived from the site office within the hour, his suit still trailing dust from the crawler. He looked at the fragment for a long time without speaking, then looked at the core logs on Kolev's screen, then at the three men standing around the bore. When he spoke, his voice on the open channel was quiet and even. The core would be logged as a bit failure in fractured basalt. Nothing would go to the colonial council in Arsia, and nothing would go up the line to the UN Space Authority. Bolo held the surface lease on this stretch of the basin, and if the company was going to claim what lay below it, the claim would be filed by Bolo's lawyers on Bolo's schedule, before anyone else knew there was anything here to claim. Kolev's jaw worked inside his helmet, but he nodded, and he began rewriting the log while Vavran watched.

No one watched Markus. While the foreman typed and the executive read over his shoulder, Markus bent to clear the core barrel, and a sliver of the black lattice, no longer than his thumb, went from the tray into the thigh pocket of his suit. He sealed the flap without looking down. It did not go into the sample registry, and it did not go into the crate Vavran ordered locked and loaded onto the crawler. Markus stood straight again with his hands empty and his face blank, and he thought about his contract, about the eleven years still owed to Bolo Extraction, and about how much a piece of something no one had ever seen before might be worth to the right buyer, far from the Hellas Basin. Against his leg, through the layers of his suit, the sliver stayed warm.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="6dc98acd-985a-472e-8dfa-faa8fdae69f3">Markus stayed by the bore at dig site 7, the warm sliver sealed in his pocket, while the drill's readings travelled up the line to Bolo.</li>
<li edge="d2c40953-630d-4bde-b602-03a049db30d1">Markus rode the Victoria down to the landing pad, where the colonial council hall stood beside the ice field.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="3576314c-ecdc-4b2a-8cd3-a02fd8087c0b" -->

The drill at Bolo Extraction dig site 7 had been chewing through Hellas Basin regolith for eleven hours when the telemetry line on Bagor Vavran's screen bent straight up. He watched it climb from his office aboard the company platform in orbit: a heat spike at four hundred meters, where nothing should have been warmer than the rock around it. He did not wait for the shift report. He opened a channel to the site and let his voice fill every helmet and every speaker in the pressurized sheds at once.

"Seal the borehole. Now. Nobody leaves the perimeter. Every one of you gets searched before you set foot on a transport." He paused, and the crew could hear him breathing, close to the microphone. "You've hit a new ore, and it's worth billions. Billions. It belongs to Bolo, down to the last grain of dust. Anyone who walks off with a crumb of it will spend the rest of their life paying it back."

Markus Vader had been standing at the core tray when the call came, and the shard was already in his glove. It was no bigger than a thumb, dark and glassy, and it was warm, warmer than his own hand, with a slow, steady heat like something asleep. He turned his back to the camera on the tray rig, peeled up the lining of his coat along the seam, and taped the shard flat against the insulation with three strips of gray sealant tape. Then he pressed the lining down, smoothed it with his palm, and went to stand in line with the others while the borehole cap hissed shut behind them.

The search was thorough and stupid. Two security men from the orbital platform emptied pockets, ran wands over boots and sample cases, cracked open thermoses, and made the crew strip their suit liners down to the inner layer. They waved the scanner over Markus's coat twice, saw the warm reading, and passed it as body heat. One of them told him to move along. He walked up the ramp of the Victoria with the shard resting against his ribs, buckled into a seat by the bulkhead, and felt it pulse faintly through the fabric as the engines came up. Somewhere above, Bagor Vavran would be filing claims and drafting press notices about his billions. Markus closed his eyes as the basin dropped away beneath them, certain that the man had no idea what he was trying to own.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="f15ce76b-0bbb-401e-9328-0c1968ea415a">Markus Vader rode the Victoria down onto the Hellas ice field, where Bagor Vavran was waiting at the Bolo drill site.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="65ebaf38-c7d0-4611-b902-4ec58095003e" -->

The inner door of the Bolo Extraction shaft airlock groaned through its last cycle, and Dmitri Kolev's crew came out still sealed in their suits, frost ghosting off their shoulders in the warmer air of the staging bay. They moved in a tight knot, helmets on, saying nothing. As they passed the tool racks, the youngest of them let a data chip slip from his glove into the open hand of Tomas Reyk, who had been leaning against a cargo sled as if he had nowhere better to be. Reyk closed his fingers, pushed off the sled, and was gone into the access corridor before the last helmet came off. Within the hour every public screen on Ceres was showing the drill footage: a bore-camera sinking through gray regolith until the rock gave way to something smooth and ribbed and plainly made, curving off into the dark beyond the lamp's reach. Over it, Reyk's voice told the colony that this lay under settler parcels Bolo had swept into its lease, and that the company had known.

In the corridor outside the bay, where the airlock's cycle lamps threw amber light across the deck plating, Markus Vader stepped into the path of Dr. Elen Markovsky. She still had her lecture slate under her arm and the tired patience of someone who had spent two hours answering undergraduates. He did not waste words. He unwrapped a strip of insulating cloth and held out a shard the length of a finger, faintly translucent, its facets catching the amber light in ways that did not quite agree with one another. She glanced at it and almost laughed. Grown quartz, she said, a bismuth trick, the kind of thing sold to miners' children in the ring markets. He asked her to hold it anyway.

She took it to humor him. Within a few breaths the shard warmed against her palm, gently at first, then as warm as skin, and while she watched, a new branch of lattice pushed out from one edge, fine as frost on a visor, crystallizing in hard, deliberate steps until it stood a few millimeters proud of the rest. Markovsky did not drop it. She stood very still in the corridor and stared at her own hand. When she spoke again her voice was lower. She would look at it in her lab tonight, after the annex emptied out, and she would tell no one. Vader asked about her husband, whose face read the colony news every evening. Not him either, she said, and wrapped the shard back in its cloth herself.

Behind them, the bay's wall screen cut from Reyk's footage to Colonel Ines Haldane, standing before the government seal in full uniform. She called the broadcast evidence of an unregistered structure of possible strategic significance beneath state-administered ground, and she demanded an emergency council hearing to assert the state's claim before any company could move another meter of rock. The crew, half out of their suits, watched her in silence.

Bagor Vavran did not watch. He had found Kolev by the suit lockers and stood close enough that the others pretended not to hear. The footage was fake, Bagor said, a staged trick, and Kolev would swear to it on record by morning, or he would be off the payroll, and the company's medical coverage for his daughter would end with him. He named the clinic. He named the monthly sum. Kolev hung his helmet on its hook, unlatched his collar ring, and looked at Bagor for a long moment. Then he turned back to the locker and went on peeling off his suit, and he did not say a single word.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="2b66a696-2346-456b-beec-f136b2ecfa79">Markus Vader went back alone to his rented room in Whitechapel, in London.</li>
<li edge="e36d53bd-3143-496a-aa71-6d1691710597">Dr Elen Markovsky went out to the supply depot at Bolo's drill site in Mars's Hellas Basin.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="58e57e44-f79f-4f74-9870-3b6b076125ae" -->

The call came through at a quarter past two in the morning, and the cracked screen of Markus Vader's tablet lit the whole of the rented room in Whitechapel: the sagging cot, the kettle on the windowsill, the damp bloom spreading across the ceiling plaster. Bagor Vavran's face filled the frame, too close to his camera, his jaw working before the audio caught up. "Sample MR-114," he said. "Logged at the dig, sealed, manifested. And now it is not in the crate. You were on that manifest, Markus. Your name. Your signature." Behind him something metal was being dragged across a concrete floor. "You understand what happens to men whose signatures go missing with my property?"

Markus kept his hands flat on the table where Bagor could see them. He swore he knew nothing, swore on his mother, on his sister's children, on every saint he no longer believed in. He said the crates had been sealed before he ever touched them, that customs had opened half the shipment at Rotterdam, that anyone could have taken it. Bagor listened without blinking, then leaned back from the lens. "Bolo's people are on your street," he said. "They have been since Tuesday. Think carefully about what you remember by morning." The screen went black, and the room went with it.

In the dark, Markus noticed the light. It came from the coat hanging on the back of the door, a faint reddish glow seeping through the wool at the hem, and when he crossed the floor and drew the shard out of the inside pocket it lay in his palm no longer than his thumb, dull and rust-coloured, brightening and fading, brightening and fading. He held his breath and felt his pulse thudding in his ears, and the glow kept exact time with it. He set the shard down on the table and stepped back. It went on pulsing, slower now, slowing as his heart slowed, as though it were listening to him from across the room.

He worked quickly after that. He tore the lead foil from the lining of an old X-ray apron he had bought for nothing at a market stall, wrapped the shard three times until no light showed through the creases, and unpicked the seam inside the left breast of his coat with a razor blade. His stitches were crooked, the thread doubled and knotted with his teeth, but when he shrugged the coat on the weight sat flat against his ribs and nothing glowed. At the window he counted two men in the doorway of the shuttered kebab shop opposite, one of them smoking, both of them watching his door. He went out the back instead, over the yard wall and through the gap in the fence behind the bins, and dropped into the alley with his heart hammering against the lead.

He walked east toward Elen's lab with his collar up and his hands in his pockets, keeping to the streets where the lamps had failed. Bagor wanted the shard back, and Bagor would not stop wanting it, and a man with nothing to give was a man with nothing to bargain with. But a man who knew where the thing was, who held the only key to it, who could name his price and walk away when it was paid, was a different man entirely. Markus pressed his palm against the stitched seam over his chest, felt the small hard shape beneath, and quickened his pace through the empty streets.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="6621129a-5c86-459d-a20a-370a6e4ba6de">Markus remembered the night the shard first came up at the Bolo drill site in Hellas Basin.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: character id="883db39f-6217-47c1-820a-b3771df93ce9" title="Bolo" -->

Bolo is a former Albanian dockworker turned debt collector who runs a crew of street watchers and enforcers across East London, and he has owed Bagor Vavran his loyalty ever since Bagor paid off the men who once wanted him dead. When sample MR-114 went missing, Bagor gave the job of watching Markus to Bolo, who means to find the thing before anyone else does, for Bagor and to settle his debt for good.

<!-- cell: character id="290d8b0f-af51-40f7-ba1b-77b1d0e0dc3a" title="Elen" -->

Elen is a materials scientist who lost her university post over falsified-data allegations she has always denied, and now runs a cash-only analysis lab out of a converted print shop in Stepney, testing whatever the city's smugglers and collectors bring her without asking where it came from. Markus once paid her to authenticate artefacts from Bagor's digs, and she is the one person he trusts to tell him what the shard is and what it is worth. She takes it on partly for the fee but mostly because a sample that answers to a human heartbeat could be the discovery that wins back the career she lost.

<!-- cell: markdown id="63caf79d-b5ce-4859-b271-cf58190037a0" -->

The survey tent smelled of hot circuitry and the fine red dust that got into everything in the Hellas Basin, even through the double seals. Dr. Elen Markovsky waited until the ground scanner finished its slow sweep before she drew the shard from inside her jacket and laid it on the steel table beside the display. It was no longer than her thumb, dark and glassy, and for a moment it sat there like any other stone. Then it warmed. A faint amber light moved through it, faded, and returned, and on the scanner a pale smear of heat deep beneath trench seven brightened in the same rhythm. Tomas Reyk leaned in from the tent flap, and Dmitri Kolev stopped coiling cable. The smear spread as the scanner caught up with it, sharpening into straight lines and right angles, then into something far too large to be rock. It ran under the trench, under the colony's water lines, and kept going past the edge of the screen.

Reyk was gone before anyone spoke. Bagor Vavran moved nearly as fast, though he never left the tent. He keyed the colony board from the corner console, and the steady thrum of the water pumps beneath the floor plates died into a silence that made everyone look down. A second command dropped the blast doors over the main lift, and red warning lights lit along the far wall. "That holds the militia for six hours," he said. "Maybe eight." He turned to Markus Vader, who stood by the rack of breathers with his arms folded. "Your crew digs tonight. All of it. I want that trench at forty meters by dawn."

"The walls won't hold at forty," Markus said. "They barely hold at twenty. You'll bury half of them and call it a delay." He swore at Bagor then, slowly and in detail, in three languages. Bagor waited him out and said nothing, and in the end it was Markus who looked away first. He had kept a figure written in grease pencil on the inside of his helmet for two years, the price of his contract, and it had never been closer. He pulled the helmet on, took a breather from the rack, and went out to wake his crew.

The radio on the console began to crackle with other people's business. Colonel Ines Haldane's voice came through clipped and calm, ordering her troops onto the ore rail line, and the yard foreman's protests were cut off in the middle of a sentence. A few minutes later the floor jumped under everyone's boots. Dust sifted from the tent's ribs, and a second channel filled with shouting about a side tunnel Reyk's militia had just blown open on the eastern face. Bagor listened, then pointed at Kolev without looking up. "The drill rig. Get it down the ramp now, before Haldane takes the ramp too."

Kolev nodded and pulled on his gloves. On his way to the flap he stopped beside Elen, who had wrapped the shard in a cloth and still held it in her hand, its warmth seeping through. He did not lower his voice so much as stop using it. He touched the scanner screen once, at a thin dark line that angled down from an old pump house well outside the trench, an air shaft from the colony's first winter that nobody had used since. Then he tapped his wrist, where a watch would be, and went out into the dust to fetch the rig. Elen stood looking at the line on the screen while the shard pulsed against her palm. Out in the dark the first of Markus's crew had begun to dig, and somewhere east of them the guns were already moving.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="8d3651a0-caa5-43d7-a1ca-a0fcf4eae1db">Elen followed Markus's crew out into the dark to watch the trench go deeper.</li>
<li edge="c02edcd0-408e-4dd4-8009-5b94bac624b4">Markus slipped away from the night dig toward the drill site, where the old shafts ran down into the ice.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="be15bdee-654d-4498-a75a-f593083704fb" -->

The night shift had been in the trench for four hours when the north wall gave way. It went without warning, the way Martian regolith always did: one moment a rust-dark face of compacted fines lit flat and white by the floodlamps, the next a soft hiss and a sheet of it sliding down like poured sugar. Tomas Reyes was on his knees at the base with a hand trowel and never had a chance to stand. The slide took him to the chest, then the shoulders, then folded over his helmet, and the only sign of him left was the faint orange blink of his suit beacon somewhere under the dust.

Markus Vader reached him first. He dropped the sample case he was carrying and went in with both arms, scooping and shoving, his breath roaring in his own helmet. The regolith kept trickling back as fast as he cleared it. He found the edge of a shoulder ring, then the collar seal, and hooked his gloves under Tomas's arms and heaved. In the thin gravity it should have been easy, but the slide held on like wet sand, and it was three long pulls before Tomas came free, coughing over the open channel, his visor scratched opaque on one side. They fell back together against the far wall, and in the floodlight behind them the collapsed face had peeled open to show something that was not stone.

It was a curve, smooth and dark as oiled slate, bulging out of the trench wall in a gentle arc wider than a man could reach. The dust slid off it instead of clinging. Dr Elen Markovsky climbed down from the lip with her scanner already raised, and when she laid a gloved palm on the surface she pulled it back again at once. Her readout put it at eleven degrees above freezing, in a basin where the night air had dropped past minus seventy. She watched the thermal trace scroll across her screen and saw a slow rise and fall in it, regular as breathing. Then Markus helped Tomas to his feet and the two of them shuffled closer, and the rhythm quickened. Elen stepped back. It slowed. She stepped in again, and it rose to meet her.

Bagor Vavran stood at the top of the trench with the floodlamps behind him, his face invisible behind a gold visor. He did not ask whether Tomas was hurt. He said they had lost enough time already, that shoring would cost them two shifts they did not have, and that they would keep cutting down along the curve until they found an edge to it. Elen started to object and he cut across her, flat and unhurried, and repeated the order word for word. Markus said nothing. Down at his hip, out of the light, his gloved thumb found the record toggle on his suit log and pressed it, and a small red mark appeared in the corner of his display. He kept it running while Bagor finished speaking, and he made certain the time stamp caught the order to dig without shoring, before he picked up his shovel and turned back to the warm, slow-breathing wall.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="c8eb8fc6-14c5-4be4-82ef-9aee12fa4cae">Markus kept his suit log running and followed the warm curve down into the dark beneath dig site 7, with Elen close behind him.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="eef8ec02-d2b3-4c79-bf8b-d898effe0d03" -->

The breach charge had left the chamber wall in a fan of red shards, and Markus Vader was the first through, helmet lamp sweeping across a vault no wider than a chapel. At its center sat the core: a domed shell the height of a man, seamless, ringed by a lattice of dark filaments that ran into the floor like roots. Where the light touched it, the shell glittered with a pale, scaled crust. Dr Elen Markovsky stopped in the gap behind him and did not move for a long moment. She had spent eleven years scraping that same crust from fossil cores under a microscope, cataloguing it as residue, as the leftover of things long dead. Here it was laid on thick and fresh, and the filaments of the lattice were faintly, unmistakably warm on her sensor readout.

"Stop," she said, and then louder, to all of them as they crowded in. "Look at the counts. The colonies in the wall are pulling back. They're withdrawing into the core. The lattice isn't a structure, it's a guard. There is something alive in there, and everything around it knows we're here." Her voice cracked on the last word. Bagor Vavran, the Bolo Mining site director, stepped past her without answering and told Dmitri Kolev to bring up the drill.

Markus turned and stood in Bagor's way. He held up his wrist unit and played back a clip, Bagor's own voice, tinny in the dust-heavy air, ordering the outer breach twenty minutes earlier while Elen's warning was still on the channel. "That goes to the oversight board," Markus said, "unless you sign my release. Now. Contract void, no penalties, no claim on me for what happens next." Bagor stared at him, then thumbed the tablet Markus held out, and Markus stepped aside, his hands open and empty, leaving the director alone with the core. Bagor looked at Kolev. "Your daughter's treatments," he said quietly. "Bolo has carried that debt for two years. Bolo can stop carrying it." Kolev's jaw worked behind his visor. Then he braced the drill against the shell and started it.

The whine filled the chamber, and over it rose three voices at once. Colonel Ines Haldane, flanked by her troops in the breach, read from a glowing sheet in a flat parade-ground cadence: by state decree the site and all contents were seized, all personnel to stand down. Tomas Reyk shouted across her that the Hellas colony held first claim, that the basin belonged to the people who lived and died in it. Bagor raised Bolo's mining charter above his head like a banner and bellowed the company's rights back at both of them. None of them listened to the others. Elen pressed her gloves to the wall and watched her readings fall toward nothing as the bit bit into the crust and threw off a spray of pale glitter.

The shot came from among Reyk's people, a single crack from a militiaman's rifle that took Kolev high in the shoulder and spun him off the drill. The bit skated, screaming, across the shell. Haldane's troops fired back before the echo had died, and Bolo security opened up from behind the spoil carts, and the chamber became a strobe of muzzle flashes and ricochets. Kolev lay against the base of the core, clutching his shoulder, his blood spotting the scaled crust, while the lattice around him seemed, in the stuttering light, to draw tighter.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="a6394a1d-3757-4fd4-8853-de311b8adcf8">Bagor Vavran turned back to the core's outer seal, and Elen and Markus followed him to its flank.</li>
<li edge="3cd412b8-1bfa-4ed0-a0fd-da6b1a0dcfb8">Markus stayed at Bagor Vavran's side by the drill, and Elen crawled toward the wounded Kolev.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="f3b0fda6-422f-4726-8993-99e03a799abd" -->

The chamber beneath dig site 7 was a pocket of cold air pumped full of the station's breath, a dome of red basalt lit by three work lamps and the blue tongue of Bagor Vavran's plasma torch. At its center the core sat in its cradle of fused rock, a lattice of dark, ribbed material as long as a man and twice as thick, its surface laced with seams too regular to be geology. Bagor knelt at its flank with his visor down, walking the torch along the outer seal in a slow line, and the sealant beneath the flame blistered and peeled back like old paint. When the cut closed its circuit there was a soft exhalation, felt more than heard, and the fog that hung near the chamber floor began to drift toward the opening.

The core answered almost at once. A low hum rose out of it, steady and deep enough to buzz in the teeth, and the lamps showed a faint sheen of condensation beading along its ribs and then vanishing as the surface warmed. Bagor pulled off a glove and held his palm an inch from the lattice, then laughed out loud. "Feel that," he said. "It's drawing. Something in there is running." He rapped the casing with his knuckles. "Self-sustaining power after however many million years. Do you know what a patent on that is worth? Billions. Not millions. Billions."

Dr Elen Markovsky did not answer him. She had already crouched at the cut with a sample blade and a vial, scraping at the gray film that had bloomed along the edge of the seal the moment the station's damp air touched it. She tilted the vial under her handlight, then scraped again a few centimeters over, and again, her movements growing faster. The residue was soft where the first sample had been brittle; under the light it showed fine branching threads, and as she watched, new threads crept out along the fresh scrape mark and joined the old ones. "It's still forming," she said, and her voice had gone very quiet. "This isn't wear, it isn't corrosion. It's growth. Bagor, the lattice isn't a machine. It's a chamber. Moisture, warmth, the seal opening. It's an incubator, and it's waking up."

Markus Vader had stood back by the ladder through all of it, the recorder clipped to his chest panel blinking its steady red, a datapad tucked under one arm with Bagor's signature glowing at the bottom of the release form. When Elen rose and reached for Bagor's shoulder, Markus stepped in and caught her wrist, not roughly, but firmly enough that she stopped. He angled his body so the recorder kept its clean line of sight on the core and on the man kneeling beside it. "Let him work," he said. "It's his claim, and he's signed for it. Every second of this is on the record." Elen pulled once against his grip and then went still, staring at the cut. Bagor had already set the torch aside and fitted a pry bar into the inner seam, and with a grunt he leaned his weight against it. The seam parted a finger's width, then two, and the hum deepened, and warm air rolled out of the gap smelling faintly of something wet and alive.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="a7d61125-bc77-4c7d-bf02-70a8b6a1443a">Markus kept his recorder trained on Bagor as the inner seam gave way beneath the pry bar.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="9ab93c52-3dc1-4b69-97f9-364d51e6e318" -->

The laboratory sat three hundred metres under the Ceres regolith, behind a door stencilled BOLO EXTRACTION — AUTHORISED ONLY, and it smelled of new epoxy and old coffee. Dr Elen Markovsky had arrived on an unregistered shuttle with her phone confiscated and a twenty-page nondisclosure agreement still warm in her memory, the kind of contract that named the penalty for breach in figures she had read twice to be sure. Bagor Vavran had met her at the lock himself, a broad man in a suit too good for a mine, and had walked her past the corridor cameras with his hand light on her elbow. Now he stood behind her in the dim room, arms folded, while Dmitri Kolev set a sealed tray on the bench: six grey-green scrapings, each no bigger than a fingernail, cut from the structure his crew had struck at the bottom of Shaft Nine.

Elen worked for four hours in the slow Cerean gravity, where every dropped pipette drifted down as if reluctant. The material was not rock. Under the scope it showed a lattice too regular for any mineral she knew, and in the hollows of that lattice something moved. She ran the stain twice, then a metabolic assay, then sat back and watched the readout climb as the sample drew down the oxygen in its chamber. When she turned around her voice was steady, though her hands were not. "They're alive," she said. "Microbial, or something like it. Respiring. These aren't fossils, Mr Vavran. This has to go to the UN Space Authority today. There are quarantine protocols for exactly this."

Bagor did not raise his voice. He did not need to. He reminded her of the contract, clause by clause, and told her that the Authority would hear nothing, not today and not ever, unless Bolo chose to tell it. Then he turned to Kolev and gave his orders like a man reading a shopping list. The crew in Shaft Nine would stay sealed below: no shift change, no surface leave, no outside comms, rations sent down the cargo lift. Meanwhile the company would see to the council inspectors, whose prices were already agreed, so that the colonists' settlement claim and the government's survey claim on that ground would founder in paperwork until Bolo's own title was beyond dispute. Kolev listened with his jaw set and said only, "Understood," though his eyes went once, quickly, to the tray of living scrapings.

Nobody in the lab had noticed that the shaft intercom on the wall still showed a green light. Kolev had left the channel open when he called up for the samples, and eleven miners crouched in the warm dark of Shaft Nine had heard every word. They heard that they were to be shut in with whatever they had cut open, that the scientist wanted them quarantined and the company wanted them silenced, and that men they had never met were being paid to make the problem vanish. For a long time the only sound on the line was breathing. Then the shift boss, a woman named Ayo Danjuma, keyed her handset away from the company channel and spoke to the others in a low voice. Whatever was down there with them, she said, they were not going to sit beside it in the dark and keep Bagor Vavran's secret. One by one, around the narrow chamber, the crew agreed. They would talk.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="e62f443e-540e-4ac3-a301-e619ddaa9e8b">In the dark of Shaft Nine, the crew turned toward the shaft airlock and the long climb back to the colony.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: character id="95ff9cea-cc8d-49a2-af30-57c654dd37cc" title="Ayo Danjuma" -->

Ayo Danjuma is the veteran shift boss of the Shaft Nine crew, a miner who has spent years keeping her people alive in Bolo Extraction's deepest tunnels and has learned to trust her crew far more than the company that pays them. When an open intercom lets her overhear Bagor Vavran ordering her team sealed underground beside living alien microbes and their discovery buried with bribes, she refuses to let them become a disposable secret and leads them in deciding to break their silence.

<!-- cell: markdown id="d9036975-4f59-4ac9-90e0-e0bdc1eb6ec0" -->

The council hall on Ceres had been cut into the rock beneath the old spaceport, and its low ceiling held every voice close, so that the room felt fuller than its forty seats. The lamps hummed. On the slate table lay a single printed sheet with the drill logs of Kolev's survey crew, the depth figures circled in red, and beside it a grainy image of something curved and dark rising out of the regolith where no rock should have been smooth. Dmitri Kolev sat at the end of the table with his hands folded too tightly. Everyone in the hall knew his men had talked in the canteens and the ore queues, and that by the morning shift half the colony had heard there was a thing buried under Sector Nine that nobody had put there.

Bagor Vavran was on his feet before the chair had finished calling the session open. He held up the Bolo Consolidated mining charter, its seal catching the lamplight, and slapped it flat on the table. "Every cubic metre of that sector is leased to Bolo," he said. "Whatever my drills touch belongs to my company. That's the law, written and stamped." Colonel Ines Haldane didn't raise her voice in reply. She unfolded a decree of her own and read it in the clipped cadence of a soldier reading orders: by authority of the Inner Directorate, any object of non-human or unknown origin was declared a strategic asset, subject to immediate seizure by the state. When she finished she laid it on top of Bagor's charter without looking at him. Tomas Reyk, the colony's elected speaker, laughed once, a short, ugly sound. "Neither of you lives here," he said. "Ceres dug it up. Ceres keeps it. The colony holds that chamber, and the colony will decide."

Dr Elen Markovsky had been waiting with a sample case open on her knees, and now she stood and pushed it into the middle of the table, between the papers. "Listen to me," she said. "I took cores from the outer surface and from a fracture along its seam. There are microbes inside it. They're alive. They respond to heat and they're metabolising something we haven't identified." She looked from face to face. "You can't lock this away from each other. It needs biologists, it needs careful handling, it needs all of us. Share the access, or you'll kill whatever's in there before anyone understands it." Bagor was already speaking over her about extraction rights. Haldane checked the time on her cuff. Reyk was counting votes under his breath. No one so much as glanced at the vials.

The hearing ended without a motion. Haldane left first, her boots loud on the stone, already murmuring into her collar to someone at the garrison. Bagor gathered his charter and went out the other door with two Bolo foremen close behind him, and through the doorway he could be heard telling them to wake the security crews and issue rifles. Reyk stayed long enough to tell the clerk to summon the militia captains to his office, then went too. Kolev sat motionless at the end of the table, staring at the circled depth figures. Elen closed her sample case slowly, latch by latch, and stood alone in the humming room while, somewhere below them all, three bands of armed men began moving toward the same chamber.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="ddb837e2-87db-4d67-b07a-7134ab1a72b9">Elen took her sample case and went to the survey tent at dig site seven in the Hellas Basin.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="7491193b-6e95-47e5-a0d3-5ca80bce8b50" -->

The artifact chamber lay deep in Ceres, a hollow of dark ice and older stone. Its walls curved too smoothly to have been cut by any drill. Dr Elen Markovsky reached it first, alone, her suit lamp throwing a narrow cone across the floor. The thing at the center was taller than she was, a column of something that was neither metal nor rock, ribbed like the inside of a shell and translucent where the light struck it. Inside, suspended in channels no wider than a hair, were the microbes. She raised the recorder in one hand and moved the lamp with the other. Where the beam touched them, the colonies brightened and dimmed in slow, rolling waves, like breath. When she drew the light away they went still. When she brought it back they pulsed again. She kept the recorder steady and whispered the timestamp into her helmet mic, and her own voice sounded very loud.

Then boots rang in three directions at once. Bolo Corporation security came through the eastern passage in grey hardsuits, rifles shouldered, with Bagor Vavran walking behind them as though he were entering a boardroom. From the south tunnel came Colonel Ines Haldane's troops, moving in pairs, splitting to either side of the arch with the practiced spacing of people who expected to be fired on. Tomas Reyk's militia filled the third entrance last. Their armor was mismatched and their weapons were patched together, but none of them hesitated. Within a minute every way out of the chamber was held, and Elen stood between the artifact and all of them with her lamp still lit.

"Kolev," Bagor said, without looking away from the column. "Set up the corer. I want a sample in the bag and logged under the Bolo seal before anyone files anything." Dmitri Kolev unslung the drill rig from his back and began bolting its legs to the floor. His hands were quick but not quite steady. Haldane's voice cut across the chamber, flat and carrying. "That object is under Coalition jurisdiction. Anyone who puts a tool to it will be shot." From the western arch Reyk laughed once, without humor. "Coalition, Bolo, it's all the same to us. Belt hands found this. Your man touches it, he's the first one down."

Kolev thumbed the drill to idle. A low hum went through the floor, and Elen felt it in her teeth. In the column the slow pulsing broke apart. The microbes pulled inward from the outer channels, bunching toward the core in tight, dark knots, and the glow guttered like a flame in a draft. "Stop," she said, then louder: "Stop the drill. They're reacting to the vibration. They're pulling away from it. Look at them." Nobody looked. Bagor told Kolev to proceed. Haldane ordered her people to aim. Reyk shouted something to his militia that set every barrel at the western arch swinging toward the center of the room. Elen's warning disappeared under three overlapping voices. The drill whined up toward cutting speed, and on every side of the chamber the safeties came off with a sound like small bones breaking.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="ba0a1d00-7d0b-44ec-8f5d-a145c13e8a88">The story followed Markus Vader through the breached wall into the chamber beneath dig site 7 in Mars's Hellas Basin.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="a0c14905-bc66-4aba-94bd-7943405fc9bb" -->

The artifact chamber lay deep in Ceres's ice mantle, a cavern of grey-blue rime cut so smoothly that the work lights slid across its walls without a single shadow. At its center the artifact rested on a shelf of black rock: a long, ribbed ovoid the color of wet slate, its seams faintly lit from within by a green so pale it seemed less like light than a suggestion of it. Dr Elen Markovsky knelt before it with her sensor array spread across the frozen floor, her helmet lamp pushed back. On her slate, a trace pulsed in slow, uneven rhythms that no machine would have produced: metabolic chatter, the chemical breathing of something alive inside the stone.

The first shot came from the eastern tunnel, where Bolo security in orange-banded armor had dug in behind cargo crates stenciled with the corporate sigil. Colonel Ines Haldane's troops answered from the main shaft, grey-suited and disciplined, firing in measured bursts while the Colonel's voice cracked across every open channel ordering the chamber cleared in the name of the Belt Authority. From the western galleries, where the ice had been hacked rough by hand tools, Tomas Reyk's colonist militia poured fire into both, their mismatched rifles flashing, Reyk himself bellowing that Ceres belonged to the people who had bled to live on it. In the faint gravity, spent casings drifted upward and hung glittering in the lamplight, and chips of ice blown from the walls fell so slowly that they seemed to be snowing.

Elen did not look up. A round struck the rock shelf a meter from her hand and the trace on her slate stuttered, then surged, a sudden spike of activity like a flinch. The green light in the artifact's seams brightened once, sharply. Then the ribs along its surface began to move, sliding together with a grinding hum that rose through the floor and into the soles of every boot in the chamber. Plates folded over plates. The seams narrowed to hairlines, then vanished. The green drained out of it all at once, and what remained on the shelf was a smooth, featureless lump of dark stone, cold under the work lights.

On Elen's slate the trace flattened into a single unbroken line. She tapped it, recalibrated, pressed a probe directly against the stone, and watched the line stay flat. Around her the firing faltered, troopers and militia alike lifting their heads above cover to stare at the thing they had been killing one another for. Haldane lowered her sidearm. Reyk said nothing at all.

Bagor Vavran shoved past his own security detail and pushed out into the open, his armored bulk lumbering in the low gravity, one gloved finger stabbing toward the shelf. "It's mine," he shouted, his voice ringing off the ice. "Bolo holds the claim. Bolo holds the survey rights. That stone is my property, every gram of it!" He went on shouting it while the drifting casings settled slowly back to the floor and Elen sat motionless beside the dark ovoid, her slate showing nothing but a flat green line.

